    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p6_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nCORE Safety Rules\n1. Rights and Responsibilities\nAll employees must make certain that:\n • Job tasks are performed only by individuals who are qualified and authorized. \n • Job briefings/task assessments are conducted before performing work and when \nactivity and/or conditions change. \n • We advise and redirect to safe work practices anyone observed performing an \nunsafe act. \n • We immediately inform each other of unsafe conditions, correct/protect it and \nreport it to a supervisor. \n • Accidents, incidents, including injuries, and related information are immediately \nreported to a supervisor. \n • Warning signs, posted instructions, placards, and/or barriers marking restricted \nareas and lockout tagout areas are displayed and complied with at all times. \n • We do not participate in horseplay or physical altercations while on Company \nproperty or participating in Company activities. \n • One person does not engage in work activity that by legislation or Company \npolicy requires two or more people. \n • A copy of the function specific safety rules must be accessible while on duty. \n • We do not possess or use a firearm on Company property unless authorized by \nCPKC Police Services. \n2� Substance Abuse\n • Inappropriate substance use is prohibited as identified in CPKC Drug & Alcohol \nPolicies. \n • Do not smoke/use electronic cigarettes in company buildings, vehicles, and \nmachines.\n • For more information see CPKC HR Policy. \n3� Non-CPKC Personnel\nEmployees responsible for non-CPKC personnel must: \n • Understand the terms of the Minimum Safety Requirements for Contractors \nworking on CPKC property, follow access control procedures, and ensure they; \no  Receive a site-specific safety orientation and job briefing. \no  Wear PPE according to location-specific requirements. \no  Do not foul track without proper protection. \no  Have a Contractor Site Safety Plan/Emergency Information Sheet. \no  Have appropriate identification. \n4",
      "order": 5,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 6,
        "label": "Page 6"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p7_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n4. Emergencies and Derailments\n • Follow all notification procedures as outlined in location-specific Emergency \nResponse Plans. \n • Approach an emergency or a derailment site only when safe to do so. \n • When arriving at an emergency site, contact the On-Scene Response Coordinator \nor Person-In-Charge for a job briefing and follow all procedures outlined in the \njob briefing. \n • Following an accident or incident, immediately notify a supervisor/dispatcher and \ndo not contact human remains, blood, or other fluids.\n5� Fire Safety\n • Maintain unrestricted access to, and be familiar with the location of, fire \nextinguishers, alarm boxes, exit aisles, circuit breakers, fuse panels, emergency \nexits and local emergency response plans.\n • Immediately replace or report any defective, discharged or missing fire \nextinguishers. \n • Store flammable and combustible material away from ignition sources.\n • When qualified, use proper grounding and bonding techniques to prevent static \nelectrical charge when dispensing or transferring flammable liquids. \n • Do not leave open flames unattended. \n • Dispose of flammable or combustible materials according to established \nCompany guidelines.\n • Upon discovery of a fire, if safe to do so, extinguish the fire. Employees must \nimmediately activate the fire alarm (where available), and promptly report the \nfire to supervisor, dispatcher and/or proper authority.\n6� Work Environment\n • Keep all work areas clean, orderly, and free from clutter and debris. \n • Must properly dispose of refuse in appropriate and approved receptacle or  \nwaste bin. \n • Employees who use needles, lancets and medical sharps must properly dispose of \nthem in sharps containers, if provided, or remove them from company property \nfor disposal, if not provided. Do not throw needles, lancets, and medical sharps \nin company trash.\n • Use approved products to wash equipment, clothing, or floors. \n • Do not use compressed air or gases to remove debris from body or clothing. \n • Do not walk under suspended loads. \n • Work materials must be kept neatly and not stored above shoulder height unless \nthe area is designed for palletized equipment/materials with a specified load \nlimit and a forklift is utilized.\n • Walkways must be kept clear to eliminate tripping hazards.\n5",
      "order": 6,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 7,
        "label": "Page 7"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p8_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n7� Vehicles, Materials, Equipment, and Tools \n • Operate, maintain, and repair vehicles, equipment, tools, and materials only \nwhen qualified, properly licensed and authorized. \n • Use CSA B376 or equivalent UL/ULC (CAN) or Department of Transportation \n(DOT) (US/Mexico) approved, properly marked containers when storing or \ntransporting flammable liquids or materials. \n • Use approved tools, equipment, and materials for the purpose(s) intended. \n • Do not modify or override safety devices or remove safety guards. \n • Maintain, repair, and store equipment according to prescribed instructions. \n • Inspect all vehicles, equipment, tools, and related safety devices for unsafe \nconditions before use. \n • Repair or tag and remove from service if defective. \n • Use three-point contact when getting on or off any equipment, machinery, \nladder, or vehicle. \n • Face the vehicle or equipment/track unit when getting on or off. \n • Ventilate exhaust when operating internal-combustion equipment inside \nbuildings or enclosed structures. \n8. Office Safety\n • When sitting in a chair, keep all chair legs or casters on the floor. \n • When not in use, chairs shall be kept pushed in to keep walkways and \nworkspaces clear. \n • Roller style chairs are only allowed at workstations, training, and meeting/\noffice rooms. \n • Fixed leg chairs are required at ready room and lunch tables. \n • Use only commercially available stepstools that meet CSA standards (CAN)/ANSI \nStandards (US/Mexico) to access elevated locations.\n • Ensure that desk and file cabinet doors and drawers are closed when not in use. \n • When ascending or descending flights of stairs employees must utilize handrails. \n9� Use of Electronic Devices\nFor Employees governed by GCOR/OTS or CROR refer to the applicable operating rules \nand related system special instructions� \nFor employees not governed by GCOR/OTS or CROR refer to the CPKC Use of Electronic \nDevices Procedure for applicable instructions. \n6",
      "order": 7,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 8,
        "label": "Page 8"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p9_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nEngineering Rules and Safe Work Procedures\nE-1 Job Safety Briefings\n1. All individuals involved in the job or tasks, who are in the work area, must \nparticipate in a job safety briefing before beginning work. An updated job \nsafety briefing must be conducted anytime conditions change. The Employee \nin Charge (EIC) will lead the job briefing and will record the briefing in the \nEngineering Job Safety Briefing Booklet. \nThe job safety briefing includes the following:\n • a discussion of the general work plan, \n • task to be performed, \n • each employee’s individual responsibility including identification of the EIC, \n • hazards recognized, evaluated, and controlled (R.E.C), \n • the type of track protection, including adjacent tracks, if necessary, provided \nto carry out the work.\n • working alone/lone worker protection is established as necessary. \n • location of emergency first aid equipment and qualified responders. \n • the name of the employee(s) assigned to main track switches must be \nrecorded in the Engineering Job Safety Briefing Booklet, if operation of main \ntrack switches is required. \n2. When the following criteria is a part of the job or tasks, the debrief section is \nrequired to be completed in the Engineering Job Safety Briefing Booklet. The \nEIC and affected employees must complete a debrief prior to cancellation/\nexpiration and after removal of track protection. The debrief section must \nindicate the time when the tracks were cleared/protection released to confirm \nthe following: \n • main track switches operated and restored to normal position,\n • all employees and equipment are no longer foul of the track, \n • track is safe for movements at authorized speed, \n • lockout or tagout applied and/or removed if applicable, \n • track authority has been released. \n3. Pages must not be removed from the Job Safety Briefing Booklet. Job Safety \nBriefing Booklet must be kept for 7 workdays after completion of the last page. \nE-2 Prevention of Overexertion Injuries\n1. Before work and after periods of sedentary work, perform Flex, Stretch,  \nPrevent to prepare your body for work and can also be used to mitigate injury \nand fatigue.\n2. When performing work follow the FAB 4: \n • Use a wide stance: A wide base of support can be on two feet, or two knees, \none knee and one foot or a lunge stance with one arm resting on a leg.\n • Bend at the knees: Stick out your butt. Make sure your knees and toes are \nfacing the same direction.\n7",
      "order": 8,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 9,
        "label": "Page 9"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p10_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n • Bend at the hips: Hinge at the hips and slightly contract your core muscles.\n • Contract your shoulders: Slightly contract the muscles allowing the shoulder \nblades to be positioned down and away from the ears.\n3. When lifting use the following safe lifting procedures:\n • Before lifting, estimate the weight of the object by performing a test-lift on \none corner.\n • Obtain assistance or lighten the load if it is too heavy to lift it safely with  \none person.\n • Before lifting, carrying, or lowering objects using two or more people, \nperform a job briefing and confirm all participants understand the \nmovements to be made.\nE-3 Prevention of Slips, Trips and Falls\n1. To prevent slips, trips, or falls, employees must:\n • Plan and choose routes that afford the safest walking conditions.\n • Keep a clear view of your route.\n • Keep hands out of pockets while walking.\nE-4 Vehicles Used for Company Business\nPre - Trip Requirements \n1. Prior to the movement of a vehicle, the driver must conduct a 360-degree safety \nwalk around of the vehicle to identify potential obstructions or hazards. \n2. Inspect vehicles for unsafe conditions before use and record in the Vehicle, \nHi-rail, Truck Crane Daily Safety Inspection and Planned Maintenance Record \nand Daily Vehicle Inspection Report. Repair, or tag and remove the vehicle \nfrom service if defective. Report all unsafe conditions or vehicle damage to \nsupervisors immediately. \n3. Drivers must know the dimensions and clearance of the vehicle being operated  \nincluding cargo. \nGeneral Operating Requirements \n4. The driver of the vehicle is accountable for the safe operation of the vehicle and \nis required to operate the vehicle in a safe and controlled manner in order to \nprevent incidents. Vehicle passengers are required to verbally advise the driver \nof risks to safe vehicle operation if the vehicle is not being operated safely and/\nor the driver is putting themselves or others at risk.\n5. Use company provided cell phones, hands free only, for limited use is approved \nwhile operating a vehicle if: It is solely for company business; It is safe to do \nso, and the vehicle is not on a public roadway in a province or state, or other \njurisdiction where such use of hands-free cell phone equipment is in violation of \nany law or regulation. \n8",
      "order": 9,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 10,
        "label": "Page 10"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p11_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n6. Personal cell phones shall not be used in company vehicles. \nExemption: Personal cell phones may be used in company vehicles if authorized \nand when a company cell phone has not been provided. \n7. All occupants must wear seat belts while operating or riding in motor vehicles \nthat are equipped with them. Exception: Refer to E-11 Riding Equipment #16 \nwhen a motor vehicle is being operated on track. \n8. Headlights/daytime running lights must be on when operating a vehicle. \n9. Do not get on or off a moving vehicle. \n10. Do not operate a motor vehicle with doors open. \n11. While transporting tools, materials, and equipment on or in a vehicle, loads \nmust be properly secured. Loose items (such as documents, tools, etc.) are not to \nbe kept unsecured on the dashboard or rear window shelf \nParking and Reversing Requirements \n12. Special care and attention is required when parking, turning or backing up \nmotor vehicles. Use pull-through parking if available. When pull-through \nparking is unavailable, unless it is unsafe to do so, back vehicles into parking \nlocations. \n13. Do not park within 6 feet of the nearest rail unless proper track protection  \nis provided. \n14. When vehicles are left unattended, they must be left as follows: \n • Secured against movement (i.e., in park, with park brake set if required, \nwheel chocks).\n • Engine stopped with keys removed. \n • All windows, compartments and doors are closed and locked. \nMotor Vehicle Hand Signals \n 9",
      "order": 10,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 11,
        "label": "Page 11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p12_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n15. If there is a passenger in the vehicle, the driver must ensure that the passenger \nexits the vehicle and assists in guiding the driver when backing up. \n16. If backing up and no signal person is available, prior to and during the \nmovement, the driver must ensure they are aware of all potential obstructions \nin their path in order to avoid contact. \n17. When backing up a motor vehicle, when a signal person is available, the signal \nperson must position themselves on the driver’s side of the vehicle if possible \nand safe to do so. \n18. If the signals are unclear to the driver, or if the signal person disappears from \nview, the driver must stop the vehicle immediately and re-establish visual \ncontact with the signal person to obtain further instructions. \n19. The signal person will use the following hand signals for backing up vehicles.\nE-5 Off Road Vehicles\n1. Only trained and qualified employees can operate off road vehicles.\n2. Use of a motorcycle, on and off road is prohibited when on duty.\n3. Inspect All Terrain Vehicles (ATV) (i.e., Honda Quad or 4- wheeler), and Utility \nTerrain Vehicles (UTV) (i.e., Kubota, Mule) for unsafe conditions before use. \nRepair or tag and remove vehicle from service if defective.\n4. Employees operating ATV’s must wear an approved DOT or SCT (Mexico) \napproved helmet.\n5. Do not exceed yard speed limits. Obey all posted signage.\n6. Operate ATV/UTV’s with headlights/daytime running lights and beacons at all \ntimes, if equipped.\n7. Cross tracks only at grade level crossings.\n8. UTV operators and riders must wear a hardhat.\n9. Do not ride sidesaddle on ATV’s or UTV’s.\n10. Seatbelts must be worn, if so equipped.\nE-6 Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) and Clothing \nOnly use Company provided approved PPE, when on duty. \n1. Use, wear and maintain PPE and clothing obtained from CPKC approved  \nlists and sources required by job task and/or work environment (including off-\nsite industries). \n2. Confirm that PPE and clothing are in working condition before use. Remove \nfrom service and advise your manager/supervisor if it is defective. Modifications \nnot allowed by the manufacturer are prohibited.\n3. Use approved PPE and clothing for the purpose(s) intended and refer to \nAppendix 2: Personal Protective Equipment and Clothing Charts. \n10",
      "order": 11,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 12,
        "label": "Page 12"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p13_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nPPE and Clothing \n4. When working in other than an office environment, wear ankle-length pants \nand waist-length shirts with a minimum quarter-length sleeves at all times. \nAthletic clothing and workout type attire including yoga pants, sweatpants, \nwindpants, shorts, halter tops, tank tops, flip-flops and sandals are prohibited. \n5. Clothing must not interfere with vision, hearing or use of hands and feet. \nHoods, if worn, must be pulled back so as to not interfere in any way with the \nemployee’s field of vision. Clothing must fit appropriately and/or be secured to \nprevent catching in machinery or on equipment. Hammer loops are not allowed \non clothing. \n6. Wear protective clothing when the potential for chemical or physical injury to \nthe body exists. \n7. Jewelry will not be worn while on duty. \n8. Long hair must be tied back or secured. \n9. Clothing must be appropriate to the conditions in the field.\nEye and Face Protection \nFor more information reference: CPKC Eye and Face Protection Procedure\n10. Safety eyewear used on CPKC or customer property at a minimum must: \n • meet CSA Z94.3 (in Canada) and ANSI Z87.1 (in the US and Mexico); \n • have permanently attached side shields; \n • not contain polarized or transition lenses; \n • not have mirrored finishes; \n • tinted lenses must be true grey and meet company tinting standards. \n • not tinted more than 90%;\n • tinted lenses not to be worn at night, not to be worn inside buildings/tunnels \nor when visibility is reduced due to low light in adverse weather condition; \nand\n • the use of red or color correcting lenses is not permitted. \nEmployees performing work overhead that have the potential to disturb or to \ngenerate dust or debris that can drop into your eyes or face must wear sealed \nsafety eye wear or goggles, while performing these tasks. \nYou are exempted from wearing approved safety eyewear in: \n • office areas; \n • designated lunch break and parking areas; \n • In vehicles with windows closed, unless you are performing repairs or \nmaintenance work; and \n • areas where exemption has been granted based upon a risk assessment that \nindicates an adjustment to the personal protective equipment requirement. \n11",
      "order": 12,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 13,
        "label": "Page 13"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p14_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nFootwear \nFor more information reference: CPKC Safety Footwear Procedure\n11. Safety footwear, used on property at a minimum must: \n • meet CSA Z195 Grade 1, Green Triangle (in Canada), ASTM F2413 (in  \nthe US) and NOM-113-STPS-2009 (in Mexico); \n • have puncture resistant soles;\n • have an 8” upper as defined by the manufacturer; \n • have a defined heel with a minimum height of 3/8 inches and a maximum \nheight of 1 inch measured from the sole except where exempted; \n • have a functioning tread a minimum 1/8 inches deep; \n • have laces or an approved closure system and be fully laced to the top and \nsnug (cannot place more than two finger widths inside the top of the boot) \nat all times to provide adequate ankle support except where exempted. \n • be maintained so they are free of tears and have a functioning tread. \n • Snake proof boots are allowed if they meet the minimum footwear safety \nrequirements.\nContact your safety specialist if an exemption is needed. \n12. Winter Footwear \n • Footwear must provide adequate protection for the cold. \n • Approved anti-slip protection must be worn when ice/snow conditions  \nare present. \n • Anti-slip spikes/overshoes must not be worn into buildings with carpet, \nconcrete or steel floors (excluding heel cleats). \n • Do not wear studded anti-slip footwear while driving unless the footwear \nis approved for use while traveling less than 25 Km/H 15 MPH on CPKC \nproperty. \n • Driving on public roads with studded/spiked anti-slip footwear is  \nnot permitted. \n • Heel cleats and abrasive grit anti-slip can be worn in all driving conditions. \n • Boots with retractable spikes must have them retracted when driving. \n\t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t\n\t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t\t\n\t\nUpper height must be\n8 inches as de/f_ined by \nthe manufacturer\nMetatersal\nArea\nProtective Toe Cap\nSole\nUppers\nHeel\nBreast of Heel Angle\n-De/f_ined Heel\nHeight\n3/8” min. and 1” max. \nTop of \nAnkle Bone\nMin. Tread Depth of 1/8”\nVamp (Front Portion of the Upper)\n12",
      "order": 13,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 14,
        "label": "Page 14"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p15_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n13. Rubber boots or waders are allowed if the boots meet the minimum footwear \nsafety requirements and working conditions require the employee to be working \nin water or mud in which lace-up work boots would be insufficient protection.\nHead Protection \nFor more information reference: CPKC Head Protection Procedure\n14. Head Protection used on property as a minimum must: \n • Meet CSA Z94.1 or ANSI 89.1 (in Canada) and ANSI 89.1 (in the US  \nand Mexico); \n • Be worn per manufacturer instructions and not be worn backwards; \n • Be fluorescent orange, yellow or white and display approved retro-reflective \nmakings; \n • Be Type 2 hard hats, to protect against rock strikes to the head, when \nworking in active rock fall zones; \n • Not be worn with anything placed between the plastic shell and suspension \nexcept for winter liners provided they are inspected prior to every use to \nensure they do not adversely affect the proper fit or function of the hard hat; \n • Not be worn with anything on top of the head that may interfere with the \nproper use of the suspension; \n • Not have baseball-style caps worn underneath a hard hat as it will not allow \nthe suspension to work properly during impact; \n • Bandanas, handkerchiefs, hoodies, toques, skull caps, stocking hats, inner \nturbans or welder’s caps that do not contain metal parts can be worn under a \nhard hat only if it is worn smoothly on the top of the head; and \n • Be free of any stickers unless approved and they are not placed closer than ½ \ninch from the edge of the hard hat and do not cover any existing hard  \nhat damage. \nYou are exempted from wearing approved head protection in: \n • Office areas; \n • vehicles or work equipment with enclosed cabs with windows and doors \nclosed; except in ballast regulators and spreaders where head protection  \nmust be worn; \n • designated lunch break and parking areas; and \n • areas where exemption has been granted based upon a risk assessment that \nindicates an adjustment to the personal protective equipment requirement. \nHearing Protection \nFor more information reference: CPKC Hearing Conservation Procedure\n15. Hearing protection used on CPKC property as a minimum must: \n • meet CSA Z94.2 (in Canada) and ANSI S12.6 (in the US and Mexico); \n • be used in accordance with Hearing Conservation Procedures and Appendix 2: \nPersonal Protective Equipment and Clothing Charts; \n • be worn anytime employees are within 100 feet/33.5 meters of running work  \nequipment or locomotive; \n • when performing cutting, welding, grinding or heating tasks. 13",
      "order": 14,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 15,
        "label": "Page 15"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p16_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nHigh-Visibility Apparel \nFor more information Reference: CPKC High Visibility Apparel Procedure\n16. High Visibility apparel used on CPKC property as a minimum must: \n • meet CSA Z96 Class 2 Level 2 (in Canada); \n • meet CSA Z96 Class 2 Level 2 CA or ANSI/ISEA – 107 Class 2 US and Mexico; \n • be visible at all times; \n • be worn zippered or snapped up at all times; \n • be obtained from CPKC approved lists and sources; and \n • be lime green/yellow in color when worn by New Hires throughout their first \nyear of service or first work season. \n17. Polyester high-visibility apparel is not permitted to be worn by Engineering \nemployees when performing work that generates sparks, flames or excessive heat. \nHigh visibility apparel does not need to be worn in:\n • office areas; \n • business rail cars and/or cars with office working spaces;\n • designated lunch break and parking areas; \n • areas where exemption has been granted based upon a risk assessment that \nindicates an adjustment to the personal protective equipment requirement; \n • when pole climbing or wearing a fall protection harness; \n • within a confined space; and \n • areas when welding and related processes are being performed. Provisions \nmust be in place to protect non-vested employee(s) from moving equipment \nwhen these tasks are being performed. \nRespirators \nFor more information reference: CPKC Respiratory Protection Procedures\n18. Respirators used on CPKC property as a minimum must: \n • meet CSA Z94.4 (in Canada) and ANSI Z88.2 (in the US and Mexico) \nstandards; \n • be used in accordance with Respiratory Protective Procedures and \nAppendix 2: Personal Protective Equipment and Clothing Charts; \n • Employees if required to wear a respirator as part of their job duties must:\no  Be fit tested by a qualified person on every model of respirator worn: \n Every two years in Canada and Mexico.\n Every year in the US.\no  Be clean shaven around the margins of the respirator to ensure a  \nproper seal.\nKnee Pads \n19. Kneepads or an equivalent must be used if performing any tasks that involve \nkneeling. \n14",
      "order": 15,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 16,
        "label": "Page 16"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p17_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nWork Gloves \n20. All employees must wear protective gloves when performing work where the \nhand could be injured or contaminated in accordance with Appendix 2: Personal \nProtective Equipment and Clothing Charts. \nHand Protection Exemptions: \n • Gloves are not necessary when working in offices or when operating  \nmotor vehicles. \n • When gloves are not worn for tasks requiring fine dexterity, Job Briefing \nHazard/Controls must be documented and appropriate controls are \nestablished to prevent exposure to hazards such as energized electrical \nsystems, sharp projections, hot surfaces or corrosive chemicals. \nFall Protection Equipment \nFor more information reference: CPKC Fall Protection Procedure\n21. All fall protection equipment used on CPKC property as a minimum must: \n • meet CSA Z259 standards Canada and ANSI Z359 standards US and Mexico; \nand be immediately removed from service if found to be defective. \nE-7 On or About Tracks\n1. Expect the movement of a train, engine, car, or equipment/track unit at any time, \non any track, in either direction, when approaching a crossing, or fouling tracks. \n2. Do not sit or lean against rail equipment, stationary equipment and/or track \nunits unless proper protection is provided. \n3. Look in both directions before: \n • Fouling or crossing any track; \n • Getting on or off equipment; \n • Operating a switch; \n • Stepping out from between equipment. \n4. Do not walk between rails or foul of track, except when duties require and it is \nsafe to do so. \n5. When walking between equipment, ensure there is a minimum of 50 feet of \nseparation between equipment. \n6. Allow at least 15 feet when passing around the end of standing equipment \nunless proper protection is provided. \n7. Maintain a safe distance from moving equipment to avoid protruding objects. \n8. Do not step on any part of: \n • a rail; \n • a switch, switch machine, or a switch heater except to operate a foot pedal; \n • a frog; \n • a derail; \n • a retarder; \n • a defect detector/hot box detector; 15",
      "order": 16,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 17,
        "label": "Page 17"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p18_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n • an Automatic Equipment Identification (AEI) reader; \n • a wayside interface unit (WIU); \n • a switch heater/blower; \n • any other trackside equipment that is not a designated walkway or platform. \n9. At night and during periods of reduced visibility, use an adequate light source \nto perform work. \n10. When traversing a bridge be aware of weather conditions, lighting conditions \nand bridge and bridge surface conditions. Walk between the rails if no walkway \nis provided. \n11. If you are located between adjacent tracks and there is equipment moving on \neach adjacent track, stop and kneel down until movement passes or stops.\n12. When duties require you to cross an unprotected track, walk directly across the \ntrack at a right angle (90-degrees). Employees must look both ways prior to each \ntrack and apply a 90-Degree principle when conditions permit\nE-8 On or About Equipment\n1. Do not cross over or cross between moving equipment or standing equipment \nthat is not protected against movement. \n2. Use designated safety devices and maintain 3-point contact when crossing \nover or crossing between equipment (i.e., crossover platforms, end platforms, \nlocomotive platforms).\nException: employees may cross over intermodal cars not equipped with \ncontinuous handholds, provided the walking platform is wide enough to cross \nsafely. Before crossing over, ensure the car is secured and will not be moved.\n3. Do not step onto a: \n • coupler; \n • air hose or train \nline; \n • striker casting; \n • journal box; \n • sliding center sill; \n • operating lever, or \n • coupler shank; \n • truck side; \n • angle cock. \n4. Do not cross under equipment. \nE-9 Material and Personnel Handling Equipment \n1. Do not walk, travel, work or stand under suspended loads and keep clear of \nswing area by a distance of two times the swing radius. \n2. Do not carry or swing loads over people or unprotected track. \n3. Do not leave a suspended load unattended. \n4. Do not exceed load capacity of material handling equipment or rigging. \n5. Do not work from a body of a truck equipped with a dumping mechanism \nunless the dumping mechanism is made inoperative, and the dump body  \nis secured. \n16",
      "order": 17,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 18,
        "label": "Page 18"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p19_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n6. Wear seatbelt while operating or riding in material handling equipment, if \nequipped with them. \n7. Prior to using material handling equipment, outriggers/stabilizers must be \nutilized as per manufactures instructions. \n8. When moving CWR in a production environment, the safe handling procedure \nincluding siren and horn usage must be followed. \n9. When handling/moving rail, use approved rail handling devices per \nmanufacturer instructions (rail tong, rail puller, rail threader, roller grip). \n10. Do not enter the bite zone of an unsecured rail, which is at a minimum one \nfoot on either side, unless it is required to, in order to perform inspection, \ninstallation, or repair.\n11. When using chain to secure loads, only use ratchet style chain binders. \nCranes\n12. Know the crane’s load limit for the configuration of the crane and rigging per \nthe crane capacity charts/rigging safe working load/working load limit tags and \nthe load weight prior to the lift. \n13. Complete Mobile Crane Capacity and Pre-lift Assessment form with any involved \nemployees prior to the job task. \n14. Cones must be used to establish the working zone prior to lifting. Cones must \nbe placed a minimum of 40’ from the radius. Employees must not enter the \ndesignated cone zone unless detailed in the Mobile Crane Capacity and Pre-\nlift Assessment form and is necessary to be present to complete the lift and is \nagreed to by the operator. \n15. Tag lines of clean nylon materials and or insulated shepherds’ hooks shall \nbe used to guide loads and allow employees to stay clear of the bite zones. \nEmployees shall not use their hands to guide lifts. \nExemption: Employees may use hands to guide the final phase of lifts requiring \na precision landing (e.g., signal mast landing on bolts) in the following \ncircumstance: \n • Lift is within one foot of the final landing location; and\n • Employees must utilize an open hand, keeping all body parts out of bite \nzones and pinch points.\n16. Do not ride on a platform, sill step, or deck of a crane in motion. \n17. Never operate crane booms, attachments or loads any closer than required and \nalways maintain at least the minimum safe distance from live electrical power \nlines (See E-19 Electrical Safety). \n18. When a truck mounted crane is in work mode, a warning cover is required \nto be placed over the steering wheel of the truck, to show that it is in work \nmode. Employees must confirm that the boom and outriggers are properly \nstowed before removing the warning cover. Secure the boom and outriggers in \naccordance with company/manufacturer instructions prior to travel.\n19. Mark the center of the load and center the load line over the load’s center of \ngravity before starting the lift, unless designed for lift off center. 17",
      "order": 18,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 19,
        "label": "Page 19"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p20_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nAerial Boom Lifts/Buckets and Elevating Work Platforms\n20. Orientated and qualified employees can operate or ride in an aerial boom lift/\nbucket or elevating work platform. \n21. Use fall protection when operating, riding or working within an aerial boom \nlift/bucket or elevating work platform in accordance with manufacturer’s \ninstructions (See E-30 Protection When Working at Heights). \n22. Stand only on the floor of an aerial boom lift/bucket or elevating work platform \nwhile in operation. \n23. Never operate any closer than required and always maintain at least the minimum \nsafe distance from live electrical power lines (See E-19 Electrical Safety). \n24. Check overhead, pathways, and clearance for obstructions. \nForklifts\n25. Only qualified and authorized employees may operate forklifts. \n26. Safety inspections and checklist to be completed before operating equipment \nfor the first time each shift. \n27. When defects are found which affect the safe operation of a forklift, the forklift \nis to be bad order tagged and immediately reported to a supervisor. \n28. Passengers are not permitted on equipment unless equipped with a passenger \nseat and seatbelt. \n29. Seatbelts must be worn at all times when in motion. \n30. Reduce speed and sound a warning at cross aisles, when rounding corners or \npassing doorways, and at any other locations where vision may be obstructed. \n31. When moving a load, keep the load as low as possible. \n32. When operating on ramps, regardless of travel, point load toward the incline, \nand empty forks point downgrade. \n33. When parking a forklift, park clear of obstructions, set the handbrake, lower the \nforks to the ground, and do not park on a ramp or incline. \n34. Always travel with the forks as low as possible, clearing the floor by 4” (10 cm) \nand no higher than 18” (46 cm) with the forks slightly tilted back. \n35. Operator must know the recommended load limit of the forklift (shown on the \ndata plate) and the capacity of the fork, and never exceed these limits. \n36. Use 3 points of contact on steps, ladders, railings, or handrails when entering or \nexiting a forklift.\nSignals\n37. When moving under the control of hand signals, regard the disappearance from \nview of the person giving hand signals as a stop signal. \n38. An operator must obey a signal to stop given by any person, at any time. \n39. When signals are used, equipment operator must take signals, other than an \nemergency stop, from only one designated person. 18",
      "order": 19,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 20,
        "label": "Page 20"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p21_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nSignal Person \n40. Must be in full view of the operator, when using hand signals and close enough \nfor the signals to be seen clearly. \n41. Must ensure a full view of the load and equipment at all times without \ncompromising personal safety. \n42. Must know the capacity and capability of the cranes used. \n43. Must be responsible for keeping all unauthorized personnel out of the area, \nover which the load will pass. \n44. Must ensure there is constant communication with the operator at all times \nusing hand signals, or by radio. \n45. Must be in view of the operator whenever any part of the material handling \nequipment is within reach of the minimum safe distance from live electrical \npower lines to warn the operator when any part of the machine or its load is \napproaching and must not perform any other duty. \n46. Prior to making a lift, update your job briefing and inform other employees in \nthe surrounding area to stay clear of the lifting and load swing zones. \nRigging\n47. When rigging for a lift, make sure that all slings, wire ropes, and other hardware \nare approved by the manufacturer for overhead lifting. \n48. If you are the rigger, you must verify that the Working Load Limit (WLL) or Safe \nWorking Load (SWL) of each component involved in a lift is equal to or greater \nthan the load it will be subjected to during the lift. \n49. Store slings in a clean, dry place. Store synthetic slings away from direct sunlight. \nStop\nArm extended, palm down, move \narm back and forth horizontally\nRaise Boom\nArm extended, fingers closed, \nthumb pointing upward\nDog Everything \nClasp hands in front of body\n \nUse Main Hoist \nTap fist on head, then use  \nregular signals\n \nSwing \nArm extended, point with finger in \ndirection of swing boom\n \nLower \nWith arm extended downward, \nforefinger pointing down, move \nhand in small horizontal circles\n \nHoist \nWith forearm vertical, forefinger \npointing up, move hand in small \nhorizontal circles\n \nMove Slowly \nUse one hand to give any motion \nsignal and place other hand \nmotionless in front of hand giving \nthe motion signal. (Hoist slowly \nshown as example.)\n \nLower the Boom and \nRaise the Load \nWith arm extended, thumb pointing \ndown, flex fingers in and out as long \nas load movement is desired\n \nUse Whipline \n(Auxiliary hoist)\nTap elbow with one hand; \nthen use regular signals\n \nExtend Boom \n(Telescoping booms)\nBoth fists in front of body with \nthumbs pointing outward\n \nRetract Boom \n(Telescoping booms)\nBoth fists in front of body with \nthumbs pointing towards  \neach other\n \nLower Boom\nArm extended, fingers closed, \nthumb pointing downward\nRaise the Boom and \nLower the Load\nWith arm extended, thumb pointing up, \nflex fingers in and out as long as load \nmovement is desired\n\t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t\n19",
      "order": 20,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 21,
        "label": "Page 21"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p22_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nInspection Requirements\n50. Inspect all new or repaired ropes, slings, and other rigging hardware upon \ninitial receipt, daily before use, whenever you suspect damage, and as specified \nper monthly and annual schedule inspection requirements. Inspections must  \nbe recorded in the machine’s log book and document the dimensions of  \nthe rigging. \nIdentification Tags\n51. All slings must have a durable and legible identification tag. If tag is not legible \nit must be removed from service. Tags must state the following: \n • Manufacturer’s name and Working Load Limit (WLL) or Safe Working  \nLoad (SWL) \n • Cable and chain slings also: \no  Size \n • Chain slings also: \no  Serial number, Grade and Reach \nE-10 Moving Equipment \n1. Only employees that are qualified and authorized are permitted to perform \nduties related to handling rail cars/equipment. \n2. Follow all location-specific policies and procedures for moving equipment. \n3. Confirm all personnel are clear prior to movements. \n4. Follow proper communication procedures. \n5. Equipment must not be left foul of clearance points. Where a clearance point is \nindicated, leave equipment beyond the clearance point. If the clearance point is \nnot indicated or visible, determine the clearance point by standing outside the \nrail of adjacent track and extend arm towards the equipment. When unable to \ntouch the equipment, leave equipment at least an additional 50 feet into the \ntrack to ensure equipment is beyond the clearance point. \nE-11 Riding Equipment and On Track Units/On Track Equipment \nRiding Equipment\n1. Do not ride between moving rail cars. \n2. Ride on the side ladder of the lead end of equipment, on the side of the \noperator if safe to do so, making sure to be clear of adjacent structures  \nand equipment. \n3. When riding on equipment, always maintain at least 3 points of contact using \nhands, crook of arm and feet.\n4. When riding a gondola equipped with drop ends, do not hold onto the  \nend post. \n5. Face the direction of travel with your feet turned toward the equipment that \nyou are riding. 20",
      "order": 21,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 22,
        "label": "Page 22"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p23_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n6. Do not ride: \n • on the deck or sill step of a crane in motion or in operation; \n • on the roof of any rolling stock or on the lading of any car; \n • between two pieces of rolling stock with a foot or hand on each; \n • on the leading end platform of a tank car; \n • on the end cage of a hopper car; \n • on the deck of a bulkhead flat/center beam; \n • in a gondola; or \n • on a flatcar. \n7. Do not use the lading of a loaded flat car as a handhold. \n8. When riding cars, detrain, ensure the derail is in the non-derailing position \nand walk past the derail location before remounting the car, when the initial \nmovement of equipment passes clear of a hinged-style derail. \n9. Avoid riding on the bottom step when going over highway crossings at grade. \n10. Ride equipment only in designated riding/seating locations. \n11. Do not occupy locomotive side walkways when operating above 25mph.\nOperating and Riding Track Units \n12. Refer to the Original Equipment Manufacture’s (OEM) Operation and \nMaintenance manuals for equipment operational specifications and safe \noperation requirements. \n13. Prior to entraining the track unit, the rider must communicate with the operator \nto receive instructions. \n14. Operators of track units must ensure riders are positioned safely. \n15. Only the prescribed number of persons can ride in track units. \n16. Wear seatbelt while operating or riding in track units, if equipped with them, \nunless it is in “work” mode and traveling less than 10 mph. Seatbelts may only \nbe removed when the field of view is obstructed and it is necessary to stand to \nobtain a clear view of the surroundings. Seatbelts are not required in hi – rail \ntrucks if engaged in on track inspections and travelling less than 10 mph. \n17. Ensure all unattended track units are properly secured from movement,  \nand doors, various compartments etc. are locked to protect against vandalism \nand theft. \n18. Lower all suspended loads and/or secure attachments before leaving track  \nunits unattended. \n21",
      "order": 22,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 23,
        "label": "Page 23"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p24_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n19. Ensure all work heads and attachments are properly secured prior to track \ntraveling track units. \n20. While operating track units; the qualified operator is permitted to handle \ncontrols directly related to the safe operation of the track unit including the \nrailroad radio pertaining to work activities. All other activities are prohibited \nwhile operating moving track units. The operator may not write or copy any \nmandatory directives/authorities/written instructions or utilize electronic devices \nuntil the movement is fully stopped. \n21. Before fouling the potential reach or turning radius of any part of a track \nunit, communication with the operator must be established and confirmation \nreceived that it is safe to be within that area. \n22. When carts are being pulled or pushed by a track unit, the carts must be secured \nwith a drawbar and safety chains. D style locking pins are required to secure the \ndrawbar to the track unit and the cart. \n23. Use of cruise control is prohibited when on track. \n24. When the work heads become jammed, obstructed or are not functioning as \nintended the track unit must be stopped and lockout tagout procedures must  \nbe followed. \n25. When work equipment is being operated, all safety guards must be in place and \nfully functional. \n26. When removed from the track, track units/track equipment (including hi-rail and \nmotor vehicles) must be placed not less than 6 feet from the nearest rail where \nthey will not be struck by passing trains, snow removal equipment or create a \nsafety hazard. \n27. When tying down equipment for movement only ratchet style binders are \napproved for use. \nE-12 Getting On/Off Equipment, Track Units and Vehicles\n1. While getting on/off stay clear of switch stands, bridge approaches, retaining \nwalls, and other fixed objects. Look where you are going to place your feet to \nensure solid footing.\n2. Do not get on or off moving equipment or track units unless working on a:\n • Shoulder cleaner; or \n • Undercutter; or \n • Automated Ditcher or \n • Get on/off equipment/track units listed above only when it is in “work” mode \nand travelling at a speed not greater than 2 m.p.h. \n3. Use 3 points of contact, with feet facing the equipment, maintaining a firm \ngrip, on steps, ladders, railings and/or handrails when getting on or off vehicles, \nequipment/track unit or structures. When carrying handheld items such as tools, \nmaterial, place items onto equipment/track unit in order to maintain 3 points of \ncontact or seek assistance prior to getting on/off. \n22",
      "order": 23,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 24,
        "label": "Page 24"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p25_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n4. Do not jump from any piece of equipment or structure to ground level or onto \nadjacent equipment or structure except in an emergency situation. \n5. Get on/off equipment and track units on the operator’s side when possible. \nE-13 Loading/Unloading Rail Cars\n1. When loading/unloading stationary cars, they must be secured by applying  \nhand brakes.\n2. When using equipment to load/unload cars, a job briefing must be performed \ndetailing work to be performed and any additional hazards/controls. \n3. If cars are to be moved during unloading/loading operations, a job briefing \nmust be performed with all involved employees, detailing movements to be \nmade, specific hazards/controls, methods of communication and employee \nspecific tasks. \nE-14 Powered Tools/Hand Tools \n1. Use approved tools, equipment, and materials, only when qualified and \nauthorized, and for the purpose(s) intended as per the manufacture’s \nspecification and instructions. Use of homemade, or non-engineered tools are \nstrictly prohibited. \n2. Run electrically powered hand tools through a Ground Fault Circuit Interrupter \n(GFCI) when using the tools outdoors. \n3. Use only electrically powered hand tools that are double insulated or grounded. \n4. Use abrasive products and wire brush wheels as specified by equipment \nmanufacturer. (See E-16 Wire Brushes, Abrasive Wheels, Blades and Stones)\n5. Visually inspect grinding wheel according to manufacturer’s specifications prior \nto every use. \n6. Protect fuel containers from ignition sources generated from grinding or cutting \noperations. \n7. When cutting with angle grinders, only approved cutting disks and angle \ngrinders equipped with kickback elimination clutches and dead man switches \nshall be used. \n8. Do not use your hands to remove chips or shavings from drills or other power \ntools. Use a brush, vacuum equipment, or special tools designed for cleaning \npurposes.\n9. Stand to the side of rotating saw blades or cutting wheels.\n10. Use a push block to feed narrow material through a circular or band saw.\nGuards\n11. Do not operate power tools, machinery, or appliances without required \nmachine/safety guards. \n12. Removing a safety guard is prohibited, except for cleaning or servicing  \nthe equipment. 23",
      "order": 24,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 25,
        "label": "Page 25"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p26_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n13. Altering or modifying a guard is prohibited, unless consistent with the \nmanufacturer’s instructions.\n14. When using a bench grinder, confirm that the tool rest is no more than1/8 inch \nfrom the wheel and that the hood or tongue adjustment is not more than 1/4 \ninch from the wheel.\nBanding, Blocking, and Bracing \n15. Assume banding is under tension when installed. \n16. Guard against personal injury when cutting banding under tension from \npackaged materials. \n17. When cutting bands, keep clear of bands and keep out of the path of falling \nmaterial. Use only band-cutters approved by the manufacturer for such use. \nWhen bands under tension are being removed from items such as cartons, \nboxes, pallets, or ties, stay away from the direct line of the bands.\n18. When removing plastic cable ties (aka: zip-ties), do not attempt to break the \ncable or zip-tie fasteners using hand techniques, cutting with pocketknives, \nor any other exposed sharp edged cutting blade instruments. (i.e.: box opener \nor utility knife). Only use approved tools, such as side cutters, diagonal wire \ncutters, or pliers to safely remove plastic cable or zip-tie fasteners. \nCompressed Air \n19. Use “anti-whip” connectors or safety cables to connect air powered tools. \n20. Regulate air pressure to ensure it does not exceed the p.s.i. rating of tools and \nequipment. \n21. If not equipped with auto lock air chucks, close the air valve(s) and relieve the \nline pressure before disconnecting air hoses, changing out tool components, or \nmoving hoses to and from storage. \nExplosive Actuated Tools \n22. Only qualified and authorized employees can use explosive actuated tools. \n23. Only load tools immediately prior to use. \nHydraulic Tools \n24. Do not use hands to inspect charged hydraulic hoses. \n25. Relieve all hydraulic pressure and discharge the accumulator before \ndisconnecting hydraulic lines. \n26. Ensure all line connections are tight and inspect for damage prior to charging \nhydraulic lines.\n27. Prior to using a rail puller/expander confirm that no one is standing on the ends \nor the open pull area of the puller in case bolts break and fly out. Keep all body \nparts out of the area between the rails being pulled.\n24",
      "order": 25,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 26,
        "label": "Page 26"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p27_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nKnives, Cutting and Stripping Tools \n28. When material needs to be cut or stripped, you must utilize approved cutting \ntools such as wire cutters, wire strippers, cable strippers, side cutters, or any \nother company approved cutting tool to avoid using a knife of any type. \n29. If a knife must be used it shall be a lock-back or self-retracting style. Use of \nknives that will not lock the position or length of the blade are prohibited. \n30. Use cut resistant gloves with a minimum ANSI Level 3/EN388 Level Cut Resistance \nto protect your hands while using cutting tools. \n31. Personnel required to use knives shall adhere to the following steps before, \nduring, and after knife use: \nPrepare: \n • Review the task at hand to determine if a knife is the appropriate tool for  \nthe job. \n • Retractable utility knives must never be extended past the first break away \npoint on the blade. \n • Always use a sharp blade. They are safer than a dull blade which requires \nmore force to cut. \n • Dispose of dull or broken blades in a puncture-resistant container. \n • Ensure the blades are properly positioned in the handle before use. \n32� Safe Work Procedure: Tools with Sharp Edges /Knife Use \nStep 1:  Position yourself so that in the event the knife does slip, it will not strike \nyourself, other employees, or equipment that are in close proximity to the \nwork area. \nStep 2:  Ensure that you have proper footing and balance before using a knife. \nStep 3:  Always hold knives by the handle. \nStep 4:  Maintain eyes-on-task while handling/using knives. \nStep 5:  Keep extremities out of the cutting path and always make the cut away from \nyourself. \nStep 6:  When possible secure the object on a solid surface to assist in controlling the \nknife’s movement. \nStep 7:  Do not exert excessive force onto the knife (ie, force in excess of that \nrequired to perform the task). \nIf any of the above criteria cannot be met, then a knife must not be used� \n25",
      "order": 26,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 27,
        "label": "Page 27"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p28_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nHand Tool Inspection \n33. Inspect all tools, and the safety devices for unsafe conditions before use. If \ndefective, repair or tag and remove from service and advise your manager. \nDo not use tools with: \nCorrect defects by dressing tools on a bench grinder utilizing a tool gauge for \nproper bevel under the direction of the Foreman. \nPrecautions while Swinging Tools \n34. Warn others to keep clear of any potential bite zones, pinch points and the line \nof fire when using swinging tools (such as a spike maul, sledge, axe, pick, hand \nadze, etc.). \n • Position yourself and ensure others assisting are positioned to be clear of \nthe striking path or zone or any pinch points if the tool is deflected or misses \nintended target. \n • Before using swinging tools, remove oil, grease, and dirt from hands  \nand handles. \n • Swing tools in controlled manner to prevent injury to self and others. \n • Refer to Appendix 2: Personal Protective Equipment and Clothing Charts for \nrequired additional PPE. \n • Do not use a sledgehammer or spike maul to strike another hammer, \nsledgehammer, or spike maul. \nStriking and Struck tool protection \n35. Striking tools must be protected against flying metal fragments when striking \nmetal on metal.\n36. All striking tools must be protected with proper sized rubber collars /shields and/\nor wrapped with duct tape prior to use. Spike mauls are excluded. \n37. Rubber collars, shields and duct tape must be inspected before and periodically \nduring use to ensure adequate protection. \nSafe Work Procedures for Hand Track Tools \n38� Spike Maul \nSpike mauls are only to be used to drive spikes.\nDo not straddle the rail when spiking. \nDo not use a spike maul to open up metal kegs. \nWhen using a spike maul: \n • Inspect the plate area and brush away any loose material that might fly  \non impact. \n • Set the spike, holding it between the thumb and forefinger, palm up. \n• Cracks or chips • Loose/missing wedges\n• Mushroomed striking surfaces • Worn ratchets/teeth \n• Burrs • Loose/cracked handles\n• Slivers • Other defects \n26",
      "order": 27,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 28,
        "label": "Page 28"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p29_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n • Firmly grip the spike maul and establish footing. \n • Keep your back straight, bend your knees and use short pulling strokes to \nprevent back injury or falling backwards. \nKeep eyes on the spike head and spike as follows: \n • Strike with light blows until the spike is firmly set. \n • Swing the maul in a smooth arc at an even rhythm. \n39� Sledge Hammer \nThe sledge hammer is used to strike other tools such as a rail drift pin, to apply \nanchors or any other struck tools. \nDo not use a sledge hammer to drive track spikes or to open metal kegs. \nDo not use sledge hammers over 16 pounds.\nWhen using a sledge hammer: \n • To strike a chisel or other approved “hammer struck” tool, make sure the \nsledge’s swing arc is at a right angle to the person holding the “struck” tool. \nWhen using a sledgehammer to install rail anchor clips: \n • Stand on the gauge side. \n • Remove enough ballast to ensure rocks do not fly \nout and strike you or co-worker. \n • Do not use excessive force when using the sledge \nto apply or remove rail anchors. \n • Gently tap the clip into position with the \nsledgehammer so that the clip is firmly set. \n • Do not drive the clip tight against the plate  \nor shoulder. \nWhen using a sledgehammer to remove rail anchor clips: \n • Stand facing the rail and restrain the clip with your foot while at the same \ntime lightly hitting the clip with a sledgehammer. \n • Do not walk in front of a person installing or removing rail anchor clips. \n40� Anchor Wrench and Clip Applications \nWhen using an anchor wrench or clip applicator: \n • Stand on the gauge side. Never straddle the rail. \n • Do not use excessive force when using the applicator. \n • Spread your feet for a stable base of support. \n • Place the wrench on the anchor and snap the anchor in place with a firm \ndownward thrust. \n • Position hands so they will not strike the opposite rail or another object if the \nwrench slips off the anchor. \n41� Rail Drift Pin \nWhen using a drift pin: \n • Insert the small end of the rail drift pin through joint bar and bolt hole \non rails to be aligned and ensure the pin comes through the bars on the \nopposite side. \n27",
      "order": 28,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 29,
        "label": "Page 29"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p30_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n • Use a sledge hammer to strike a drift pin using controlled blows. \n • Ensure other workers or persons are not standing in a location where they \ncould be struck by flying metal or with the pin in case it flies out. \n42� Spike Lifter\nWhen using a spike lifter: \n • Insert the spike lifter under the spike head and tap it with the sledge hammer \nto loosen or raise a spike enough that a claw bar can be inserted underneath. \nWhen possible continue using a claw bar. \n • Use extra caution to ensure the safety of the person working \nwith you. \n • Ensure that you are well clear of the swing zone of the  \nsledge hammer. \n • Ensure both feet are on one side of the handle and that you \nhave good footing. \n • Be aware of cut-throat spikes that may break and fly when using spike lifter \nor when applying pressure to pull with claw bar. \n43� Tie Plug Punch \n • Use extra caution to ensure the safety of the person working with you. \n • Be sure to strike the plug punch squarely with the sledge hammer. \n44� Claw Bar \nWhen using a claw bar: \n • Do not carry it over your shoulder. \n • Use a spike lifter if you cannot get the claw under the spike head. \n • A claw bar is not a struck tool. \n • Keep your top hand below the indentation to avoid pinching your fingers on  \nadjacent rail. \n • Do not use a claw bar to turn and/or roll rail. \n • Place the claw securely under the spike head. \n • Stand beside the claw bar and position your hands to keep them from \nstriking the opposite rail or another object if the spike head breaks or \nreleases suddenly; and ensure you have firm footing. \n • Work the spike up with short, firm jerking thrust motion, not continuous \npressure and watch for throat-cut or loose spikes. \n • Use a piece of wood, spike, or other suitable object under \nthe claw bar heel for additional leverage. \n • Be sure the end of the claw bar is well underneath to \nprevent slipping when using it to nip ties, tie plates, or rail. \n45� Shovel \nWhen using a shovel: \n • Do not carry the shovel on your shoulder. \n • Do not lift and twist your back when removing dirt or ballast, move your feet. \n28",
      "order": 29,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 30,
        "label": "Page 30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p31_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n46� Tie Tongs \nWhen using tie tongs: \n • Ensure the tie tong points are sharp and have a firm bite in the tie before \nmoving and tie has been loosened. \n • Brace feet before pulling to avoid falling if tongs slip.\n • Do not use excessive force, applying steady force without jerking. \n47� Lining Bar/Nipping Spoon \nWhen using a lining bar or nipping spoon: \n • To nip ties or line track, place the bar/spoon securely in the ballast to prevent \nthe bar from slipping out when force is applied. \n • Keep your back straight and your knees bent when pushing. \n • Apply force smoothly and stand firmly to maintain balance if the bar or \nspoon slip. \n • Use a tie plate or piece of wood as a fulcrum to multiply your force on the tie. \n • Do not carry the lining bar/nipping spoon on your shoulder, and never \nstraddle the tool while using. \n48� Wrench \nWhen using a wrench: \n • Always use the correct size of wrench for the job. \n • Do not strike a track wrench. \n • Pull on the wrench whenever possible. If necessary to push, do so with an \nopen palm. \n • Stand on the opposite side of the nut or bolt to be tightened or removed \nuntil the nut is snug; then move to the same side as the nut to complete  \nthe process. \n • Do not use a jerking motion. \n • Never use a pipe extension on a track wrench.\n • In close spaces, follow these precautions: \no  Position hands to avoid injury if the wrench slips. \no   Limit your stroke to about a 45-degree angle on either side of your body \nwhen tightening or loosening a track bolt. \no  Brace yourself to avoid falling if the wrench slips or the bolt breaks. \no  Do not straddle the rail. \n49� Hand Adze\nWhen using a hand adze: \n • Check the handle for splinters or cracks. \n • Ensure the handle is securely wedged. \n • Keep the cutting edge sharp. \n • Remove nails, dirt, stones, and other debris from the item to be adzed. \n • Straddle the object when possible, then work the adze between your legs, \nmaintaining good control to prevent glancing blows. \n • Cut with the grain. Notch and chip out pieces if removing a large amount. \n29",
      "order": 30,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 31,
        "label": "Page 31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p32_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n50� Rail Fork \nWhen using a rail fork or rail turner: \n • Make sure the rail is free to move and is not secured, wedged in, or covered \nwith dirt or ballast before turning it. \n • Take position at the end of the rail to be rolled and warn personnel that rail \nwill be rolled. \n • If equipment, men, tools and material are clear of the rail, then brace \nyourself, firmly hold onto the end of the tool, tilt the rail, and hold it in \nposition. Apply force steadily and keep clear of the rail in case the tool slips. \n • A second person pushes the rail off the plates by sliding a lining bar under \nthe rail and lifting. \n • Never place a tool or bar in the bolt hole to turn the rail. \n • Ensure that the pin is not cracked or bent, or mushroomed. \n51� Track Jack \nWhen using track jacks: \n • Set track jacks outside the rail, if possible. \n • Place the jack base on an even and firm surface so the jack will not overturn \nunder the load. \n • Place the lifting toe fully under the load. \n • Do not strike the track jack with tools to force it under the load. Instead, dig \nthe jack seat deep and wide enough for easy insertion. \n • Use only a lining bar or approved jack handle to operate the track jack  \nas follows: \no  Stand beside the bar and pump it in an even rhythm, making sure the \nratchet/hydraulics are fully engaged. \no  Never straddle, sit, or stand on the bar. \no  Keep clear of pinch points. \n • Remove the bar when not operating the track jack. \n • Make sure that all employees, tools, and materials are in the clear before \ntripping or lowering a track jack supporting a load. \n • Do not set track jacks for tripping until you are ready to release the load. \n52� Files: \nWhen using a File: \n • Use files with handles. \n • Do not clean files by striking them against a vise or other metal object. \n • Do not hammer files or use them as prys, punches, chisels, or any other tools.\nE-15 Welding, Cutting, Grinding and Torch Cutting \n1. Only trained and qualified employees can operate welding and torch cutting \nequipment. \n2. Inspect all welding and cutting equipment prior to and after use. \n3. Perform a leak test every time the torch system is setup. \n30",
      "order": 31,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 32,
        "label": "Page 32"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p33_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n4. Ensure that flammable solids, liquids, or gases have been purged prior to \nwelding or cutting on a container. \n5. Do not carry compressed liquid fuel lighters or matches while welding or  \ntorch cutting. \n6. Have a fire extinguisher or fire-extinguishing device, within 50 feet, when \nwelding, torch cutting, grinding, or performing any work that may produce \nheat or sparks.\n7. Do not use oxygen for ventilation or as a substitute for compressed air. \n8. Store and keep regulators free from oil or grease. \n9. Only store gas cylinders in properly vented cabinets and locations. \n10. Transport and use compressed gas and oxygen cylinders in a secure,  \nvertical position. \n11. Only use approved strikers for igniting torches. \n12. During government designated high/extreme fire risk periods, dampen \nimmediate work area with water prior to beginning work. Utilize shielding to \ncontrol the spread of sparks. Ensure firefighting tools and equipment are readily \navailable as required per the local annual fire plan. At a minimum, 1 axe, 1 \nwater pump backpack and 2 shovels, must be readily available at the work area.\n13. Direct flame or sparks away from people, equipment, and any flammable \nmaterial. \n14. Remove combustible and/or flammable items from area before beginning work. \n15. You must use a respirator and extractor/blower to protect yourself from \nmanganese particles when welding, tapering or grinding a frog casting, a \ndiamond insert, a movable bridge joint, or a rail end. \n16. Acetylene use is prohibited unless there is written exemption from a Chief \nEngineer. \n17. Inspect work area after job is completed and extinguish any hot spots, smoking/\nsmoldering ties/debris before leaving work site. \n18. Do not weld, cut or heat chains, hooks rings, links or couplings intended for \nlifting purposes.\n19. Shield concrete when welding or cutting on or near it.\nThermite Welding \n20. Do not assist in the thermite welding process unless you are qualified or under \nthe direct supervision of a qualified thermite welder. \n21. The welder foreman will ensure all non-essential people stand a minimum of  \n45 feet (15 m) away from the crucible before igniting the charge. \n22. Once the charge is ignited all remaining people must move 45 feet (15 m) from \nthe weld and remain there until the crucible has completely finished pouring. \n23. Provide safety glasses and/or face shields with a shade 4, 5 or 6 lens to people \nwatching the pouring process. If unsure use the darkest one especially at night. \n31",
      "order": 32,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 33,
        "label": "Page 33"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p34_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n24. Protect the thermite kit, crucible, molds, and molten metal from water. Do not \ndump the hot slag from the slag pans on wet soil, in water, or on snow. \nE-16 Wire Brushes, Abrasive Wheels, Blades and Stones \n1. Visually inspect all abrasive wheels, blades, and stones (abrasive products) \nbefore use. \n2. Discard abrasive products if they are: \n • Warped, cracked, chipped, or otherwise damaged in any way. \n • Contaminated with oils, grease, or solvents. \n • Exposed to freezing temperatures after becoming wet.\n • Past their expiration date.\n3. Protect abrasive products from damage during storage and use. \n • Store abrasive products in a dry area without extreme temperature changes, \nespecially avoiding below-freezing temperatures. Wet wheels may break or \ncrack if frozen. \n • Protect abrasive products from grease, oil, and solvents. \n • Do not store abrasive products on a tool unless the tool can be stored in a \nmanner consistent with the above. \n4. Use properly rated/RPM and properly sized abrasive products and wire brush \nwheels as specified by the equipment manufacturer. \n5. Inspect each abrasive product immediately after unpacking it from the shipping \ncontainer and again just before mounting it on the equipment. If the product is \ndefective or irregularly shaped, do not mount the product or otherwise place it \nin service. \n6. Perform a ring test on abrasive product as recommended by the manufacturer. \n7. Confirm protective guards are in place, secured, and properly aligned  \nbefore use. \n8. Run new wheels at full operating speed before beginning to grind or cut; most \ndefective wheels break when first used. \n9. Do not stand or walk in the direct line of a rotating wheel. \n10. Handle abrasive stones carefully to prevent dropping or bumping them.\nE-17 Jacks \n1. Jacks must be of proper capacity for the planned lift and used according to \nmanufacturer’s and or company instructions. \n2. Do not work under or place any body part under a jacked load until blocking is \nin place to support the load. \n3. Do not leave jacked, unsupported equipment unattended. \n4. Verbally notify all employees in the work area and confirm that all employees, \ntools, and materials are clear before tripping or lowering a jack supporting  \na load. 32",
      "order": 33,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 34,
        "label": "Page 34"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p35_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nE-18 Lockout Tagout - Hazardous Energy Control\nFor more information reference: CPKC Hazardous Energy Control Procedures\n1. Machines, equipment and/or processes must be made safe and isolated  \nfrom all energy sources rendering the machine and/or equipment and/or \nprocess inoperative prior to performing maintenance, repair and/or servicing \nrelated tasks. \n2. Each employee must apply a company provided personal lock and tag when \nrequired to isolate/control hazardous energy in accordance with prescribed \ninstructions. \n3. Use company provided personal locks and tags for their intended purpose only. \n4. Only the person who applies his/her personal lock and tag may remove it in \naccordance with the Hazardous Energy Control-Lockout Policy. \nE-19 Electrical Safety\n1. Only perform electrical work for which you are qualified and authorized in \naccordance with established procedures. \n2. De-energize and lockout/tagout all electrical equipment prior to performing \nwork unless the work to be performed specifically requires it to be energized \n(see E-18 Lockout Tagout, Hazardous Energy Control). \n3. Tools, testing equipment, and personal protective equipment used when \nperforming electrical work must be: \n • company approved and inspected prior to each use; and \n • operated, tested, and repaired in accordance with manufacturer instructions \nand regulatory requirements; and \n • authorized tools and test equipment to work on electrical components; and \n • rated for the voltage being worked on. \n4. Wear appropriate personal protective equipment to perform electrical work \nsafely in accordance with established procedures and the Personal Protective \nEquipment (PPE) and Clothing Charts in this rule book. \n5. Do not wear conductive items of clothing or jewelry when working around \nelectrically energized equipment. \n6. Do not operate or move any part of material handling equipment (i.e. cranes, \naerial boom, man-lifts, elevated work platforms, mobile/work equipment, etc.) \nany closer than required but never closer than the minimum safe distance from \nenergized overhead power lines (as per the Table 1). \n7. If unsure as to the voltage carried in any overhead power line, confirm by \ncontacting the appropriate company authority or electrical utility company prior \nto commencing work. \n8. Any overhead power line must be considered energized unless the authority \nowning the line or the electrical utility responsible for the line confirms that \nit is not energized and has been visibly grounded. Where the disconnect \nresponsibility is owned by CPKC lockout requirements apply (see E-18 Lockout \nTagout, Hazardous Energy Control). \n33",
      "order": 34,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 35,
        "label": "Page 35"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p36_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nPersonnel or Equipment Operating Near Overhead Power Lines\n9. When operating, no part of any equipment, crane or its load, shall be permitted \nto approach closer to any overhead power line than the distances specified in \nTable1 below, unless the line has first been de-energized and visibly grounded \nby the local electrical authority responsible for the line. \n10. Any equipment, crane, aerial boom lifts or elevating work platforms operating \nwithin the equipment’s reach of the minimum safe distance (as specified in \nTable 1 below) of any overhead power line, must have a competent signaler \nstationed at all times within view of the operator, to warn the operator when \nany part of the machine or its load is approaching the minimum safe distance \nfrom the power line. \n11. Except where electrical distribution and transmission lines have been de-\nenergized and visibly grounded at the point of work, or where insulating \nbarriers have been erected to prevent physical contact with the lines, equipment \nand cranes shall operate so that no part of the equipment, crane or load enters \ninto the minimum safe distance shown in Table 1. \n12. Prior to working near a radio transmission tower or high-voltage electrical \nequipment where an electrical charge can be induced in the crane, equipment \nor materials being handled, tests shall be made to determine if electrical charge \nis induced on the crane. The induced charge shall be dissipated by applying \ngrounding cables or other effective means before workers contact the load, and \nall flammable materials must be removed from the immediate work area. \nTable 1. Minimum Safe Distance from Live Electric Power Lines \nVoltage kV Minimum Distance  \n(meters) \nMinimum Distance \n(feet) \n0 to 50 3 10 \nOver 50 to 200 4.6 16.6 \nOver 200 to 250 7.6 25 \nOver 250 to 750 10.7 35 \nOver 750 to 1000 13.7 45 \nE-20 Chemical Safety\n1. Handle or work with a hazardous substance only if authorized. \n2. Refer to Safety Data Sheets (SDS) prior to working with a chemical. \n3. When storing or transporting hazardous substances they must be in approved \ndesignated locations and containers. \n4. When transferring a hazardous substance from one approved container to a \nsecond approved container, apply the proper workplace label to the second \ncontainer. \n5. Keep sparks, flames, and other ignition sources away from stored  \nflammable materials. \n34",
      "order": 35,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 36,
        "label": "Page 36"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p37_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nE-21 Gasoline and Compressed Gas Cylinders \n1. Secure cylinders at all times by chains, straps or cages to prevent unintended \nmovement. \n2. Transport, and store cylinders in a vertical position. \n3. Transport and store cylinders in a properly ventilated cabinet or area. \n4. Transport compressed gas cylinders with regulator removed and caps applied \nunless  transporting in tanks equipped with “all-top” protection. \n5. Tightly close all valves on both filled and empty cylinders when not in use. \n6. Store full cylinders separately from empty cylinders. \n7. Turn off vehicle engines and all other sources of ignition when fueling vehicles/\nmachines, and do not smoke or use cell phones when filling cylinders/portable \ngas containers. \n8. Use only approved containers to transport fuel. To avoid static electricity \ndischarge, remove portable containers from a vehicle and place them on \nthe ground, pump island, or concrete slab prior to dispensing flammable or \ncombustible liquids. \n9. Do not use plastic containers for gasoline where prohibited by law  \nor regulations. \n10. Ensure the fill cap and vent cap are properly seated before transporting  \nor storage. \n11. Place the container in the vehicle in an upright, ventilated position, always \nsecure the container. \n12. When filling a portable gas container do not use the gasoline pump handle lock. \n13. Refuel portable gas-powered equipment per manufacturer instructions. \n14. Keep flammable liquids and compressed gases away from ignition sources.\n15. If a cylinder is leaking, stay upwind of the location, secure the area and notify  \na supervisor. \nE-22 Dangerous Goods, Transportation\n1. Handle or transport dangerous goods only if qualified. \n2. If not trained and qualified, only handle or transport dangerous goods when \nauthorized and in the presence of a qualified individual. \n3. Ensure the proper display or removal of safety labels and placards. \n4. Ensure there is a shipping document for the dangerous goods being handled. \n5. Use approved, properly marked containers when storing or transporting \nflammable liquids or materials. \n35",
      "order": 36,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 37,
        "label": "Page 37"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p38_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nE-23 Fusees (Not applicable in Mexico)\n1. Store fusees in authorized locations, such as approved flagging kits. \n2. When igniting a fusee: \n • hold and strike it away from your body; \n • do not look directly at the fusee; and \n • do not ignite near fuel sources and be on guard for sparks. \n3. When extinguishing the fusee: \n • bury the burning end in sand or loose dirt; or \n • gently strike the burning end of the fusee over the edge of a rail or a heavy \nmetal object to ensure the burning compound is separated from the rest of \nthe fusee; or\n • let the fusee burn to completion. \n4. After extinguishing a fusee, dispose of it in an appropriate place. Do not allow \nthe burning compound to come in contact with combustible material. \n5� Safe Work Procedure: Fusees\nStep 1: Hold the fusee near the base. \nStep 2:  Pull the tape over the top to expose the scratch surface on the end of  \nthe cap. \nStep 3: Twist the cap away from the head of the fusee. \nStep 4:  Hold the cap stationary, turn your face away, and rub the igniter on the head \nof the fusee lightly against the scratch surface of the cap in a motion away \nfrom the body.\nStep 5:  If the fusee does not light, pause before attempting to light it again. While \npausing, keep the fusee pointed away from your face and body.\nE-24 3-Point Protection\n1. Only employees that are qualified and authorized are permitted to perform \nduties related to handling rail cars/rolling stock equipment.\n2. Ensure 3-point protection is provided when connected to a locomotive or a \nmachine if cars are connected and when:\n • Breaking the plane of the rail with your torso, between equipment or within \n15 feet of the end of the equipment.\n • One or both feet enter between the rail, between equipment or within  \n15 feet of the end of the equipment.\n3. When more than one employee requests 3-point protection, ensure that each \nemployee cancels the requested protection before removing 3-point.\n36",
      "order": 37,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 38,
        "label": "Page 38"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p39_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n4. To apply 3-point protection (track mobile, on track equipment,  \nand/or hi-rail equipment)\n • Apply unit brakes and automatic (train) brake if so equipped.\n • Place the unit in neutral.\n • Apply the parking brake.\nOnce the steps have been completed, communicate to the employee requesting \n3-point protection, that such protection has been applied.\n5. Do not release 3-point protection until all protected employees have released \ntheir protection.\nRequest 3-point protection any time you deem it necessary in order to perform  \na task safely�\nE-25 Coupling and Uncoupling Equipment\nCoupling/Uncoupling\n1. Only employees that are qualified and authorized are permitted to perform \nduties related to handling rail cars/equipment. \n2. Before coupling cars: \n • If riding equipment prior to coupling, detrain (if riding a locomotive, you are \nexempt from detraining); and \n • Ensure couplers are aligned and that at least one knuckle is open. \n3. Only use an operating lever under the following conditions: \n • while stopped \n • at a walking pace facing the direction of travel, never run; or \n • on the locomotive, while riding in the locomotive stairwell. \n • Use only your hand to operate the uncoupling lever.\n4. If required to adjust the knuckle by hand: \n • separate equipment by a minimum of 50 feet; \n • ensure 3-point protection is provided, confirm the slack has adjusted and no \nmovement will occur on the unattached equipment; and \n • with one foot outside of the rail, lift the operating lever with your left \nhand, grab the middle of the knuckle with your right hand and pull the \nknuckle open. \nAligning Drawbar/Coupler\n5. Separate equipment by a minimum of 50 feet \n6. Ensure that equipment is secured and 3-point protection is provided. \n7. Do not adjust coupler by kicking it with your foot. \n8. Do not lift a drawbar. \n37",
      "order": 38,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 39,
        "label": "Page 39"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p40_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n9�  Safe Work Procedure: Aligning a Coupler with Backwards or  \nForward-facing method\nStep 1:  Ensure 3-point protection is in place and/or cars are secured with a minimum \nof 50 foot separation.\nStep 2: Stand to the side of the coupler.\nStep 3:  Face or turn your back to the coupler leaning against it.\nStep 4:   Put one foot beneath the coupler, one foot in front of you or one foot \nbehind you, depending on the method.\nStep 5:   Watch your footing, especially during wet and/or winter conditions. Do  \nnot step on the rail when walking; however, it is permitted to brace your \nfoot on the rail for added leverage when aligning the drawbar when using \nthis technique.\nStep 6:  Grip the coupler from beneath and watch your fingers.\nStep 7: Keep the load close to your body.\nStep 8:  Bend at the knees and hips while maintaining a neutral spine.\nStep 9:   Rise up from the knees, using your leg muscles, and shift your weight \nforwards or backwards to push the drawbar over. Do not lift the drawbar or \nelevate your shoulders when pushing. \nStep 10:   Use whole body movements, pushing small distances, stopping, and \nrepositioning as needed (repeating the steps above) until the drawbar is \naligned. Do not overexert. \nKnuckle Replacement\n10. When removing or replacing knuckles, keep feet clear of area directly beneath \nthe knuckle.\n11. Do not insert your fingers through the hole at the bottom of a coupler to raise \nthe lock block to open the knuckle or any pinch points inside the coupler.\n12� Safe Work Procedure: Replacing Knuckles\nStep 1:  Ensure 3-point protection is in place or cars are secured with  \n50 ft separation.\nStep 2:  Obtain the same knuckle type as removed.\nStep 3:  Keeping feet clear of the area under the coupler, make sure the knuckle pin \nis in place and open the knuckle.\nStep 4: Remove the cotter pin and the knuckle pin.\nStep 5: Remove the knuckle from the coupler.\nStep 6:  Holding the old knuckle close to the body (with stable base of support) place \nit away from the immediate work area so it will not become a tripping or \noperational hazard.\nStep 7:  Drop the replacement knuckle over the rail first before repeating the  \nsquat lift.\n38",
      "order": 39,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 40,
        "label": "Page 40"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p41_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nStep 8:  Move the lock block back into the coupler recess with the operating lever \nheld up with an operating lever strap or other available item (e.g., stick  \nor belt).\nStep 9:  Use a squat technique with wide stance, bending through the hips and \nknees, keeping your back neutral as you lift the knuckle off the ground and \nplace it into the coupler pocket.\nStep 10: Close the knuckle.\nStep 11:  Insert the knuckle pin into the pinhole and cotter pin, with both hands free, \nmaking sure it locks properly.\nE-26 Air Hoses (Handling)\n1. Only trained and qualified employees are permitted to handle air hoses. \n2. Reaching over a drawbar to open an angle cock is prohibited. \n3. You may reach over a drawbar to close an angle cock provided the angle cock \ncan be reached without leaning on the drawbar or coupler and no part of your \nbody becomes exposed to any pinch point. \n4. Turn your head away from the glad hands when air hoses are being uncoupled. \n5. Never kick, strike at, or jostle the air hose(s) to stop a leak. \n6. Treat all air hoses and angle cocks as if they are under pressure. \n7. When separating equipment with a charged brake pipe: \n • close the angle cock on the equipment to be moved; \n • leave the angle cock open on the standing equipment; \n • lift the operating lever to release pin; \n • separate the equipment; and \n • observe and communicate to your crew that the equipment left standing is  \nin emergency. \n8� Safe Work Procedure: Coupling Air Hoses\nStep 1:  Ensure equipment is secure, if equipment is attached to a locomotive, ensure \n3-point protection is in place. \nStep 2: Step between the cars. Keeping one foot outside the rail whenever possible.\nStep 3: Make sure that both glad hands have gaskets. \nStep 4:  Bend at the knees and hips while maintaining a neutral spine  \n(i.e. keeping your lower back, mid-back and neck curves in proper alignment) \ngrasp the air hose nearest you firmly behind its glad hand and bend  \nit upward.\nStep 5: Grasp the hose farthest away and pull it toward the bent hose.\nStep 6:  Match the glad hands into opposite contoured slots and push them \ndownward. \nStep 7: Make certain that the glad hands are seated. \nStep 8: With your head turned away, gradually open the angle cock. \n39",
      "order": 40,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 41,
        "label": "Page 41"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p42_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n9� Safe Work Procedure: Opening Angle Cocks\nStep 1: Ensure 3-point protection is in place. \nStep 2:  With your head turned away, gradually open angle cock, bracing hose \nagainst leg. \nStep 3:  If you hear an air leak, close both angle cocks and make sure there is no \npressure in the hoses before adjusting or repairing the leak. \n10�  Safe Work Procedure: Opening an angle cock or “dumping the air” to an \nuncoupled air hose\nStep 1: Ensure 3-point protection is in place. \nStep 2: Grasp the hose at the glad hand clear of the vent port. \nStep 3: Brace the glad hand firmly against your thigh just above the knee.\nStep 4:  Turn your face away from the glad hand before slowly opening the  \nangle cock. \n11� Safe Work Procedure: Uncoupling Air Hoses\nStep 1: Ensure 3-point protection is in place.\nStep 2: Close both angle cocks. \nStep 3:  Crouching down, firmly grasp each glad hand with your hand near  \nthe coupling. \nStep 4:  With your arm extended and with a quick motion, pull upward on the  \nglad hands. \nE-27 Hand Brake Operation - Equipment\n1. Only qualified and authorized employees may operate hand brakes. \n2. Fully release all hand brakes prior to moving equipment. \n3. When operating wheel-type hand brakes, always grip the brake wheel with \nyour thumb on the outside of the wheel rim and never overexert. \n4. Do not apply or release wheel style hand brakes from the ground unless the \nbottom of the handbrake wheel is at shoulder height or below. \n5. When releasing hand brakes check at least two cars beyond the last hand brake \nfound to ensure there are no more hand brakes applied. \n6. Never use a hand brake wheel as a hand hold.\n7. Never use both hands or, a foot to operate a hand brake. Exemption: Both \nhands may be used to operate a horizontal handbrake and a foot may be used \nto operate the pawl.\n40",
      "order": 41,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 42,
        "label": "Page 42"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p43_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nE-28 Switches and Derails \nSwitches\n1. Operate switches and derails only when qualified and authorized. \n2. Keep the body, hands, and feet clear of all moving parts and the path of the \nswitch handle. Verbally notify all employees in the switch point area and \nconfirm that all employees, tools, and materials are clear before operating. \n3. Do not apply force with your foot on a switch/derail handle. \n4. Prior to operating switches, check switch rods and switch points for ice, ballast, \nor any other debris that may prevent the switch from lining freely. Check if the \nswitch has been “spiked” or “clamped.” Remove foreign material from between \nthe switch point and stock rail using a broom, stick or similar object. Do not use \nyour hand or foot. \n5. Remove the switch point lock pedal, where equipped, before operating switch. \n6. Check switch points, targets, and derails for proper alignment before and after \nhandling a switch/derail. \n7. Reapply the switch lock or keeper after the switch/derail has been used. \n8. Never use excessive force while operating a switch. \n9. Use both hands while operating the switch handle to align (move the handle \nacross the plane allowing the switch to turn) the switch points and derails. \n10. A switch must not be operated while any part of a car, engine, track unit is \nbetween the switch points and the fouling point of the track to be used. \nDerails\n11. Prior to operating a derail: \n • Ensure the track is either clear of traffic in both directions or equipment is \nsecured to prevent uncontrolled movement and; \n • Ensure that your movement is stopped 100 feet from an applied  \nmanual derail. \n12. If a lock is present, lock derails in non-derailing position when switching. \n13. Test a flip-type derail (with or without handle) for resistance before attempting \nto lift. \n14. Keep the body, hands, and feet clear of all moving parts and the path of  \nthe derail. \n15. When not in use derails and red flags must be secured to prevent them from \nbeing stolen or applied to the track to stop trains.\n16. When installing portable derails, install them to derail away from main line \ntracks.\n17. Do not install portable derails on rail with wear on gage side or on the low side \nof curves to ensure secure fastening of the derail to the rail.\n41",
      "order": 42,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 43,
        "label": "Page 43"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p44_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n18. Ensure the derail(s) are installed against a crosstie that will support lateral \nforces, if struck by rolling equipment/rail cars. \n19. Ensure portable derails are installed according to the manufacturer’s \ninstructions. Test derail once applied that it is tight and secured to the rail/tie. \n20. Portable derails must be secured with locks when applied and used with \nreflective red flags. \nE-29 Handling Chocks \n1. Work only from the side of the vehicles, equipment, or track units. \n2. Keep fingers and hands clear of pinch points. \n3. Use only an approved chocking device. \nE-30 Protection When Working at Heights \nFor more information reference: CPKC Fall Protection Procedure\nFall Protection\n1. Use fall protection when working at heights in excess of: \n • 10 feet (3.0 m) in Canada;\n • 12 feet (3.7 m) on bridges in the United States, unless trained & qualified \nto perform bridge inspections as per FRA regulations and 6 feet (1.8 m) \neverywhere else.\n • 6 feet (1.8 m) in Mexico. \n • 10 feet (3.0 m) in Canada, the US and Mexico from a portable ladder when \nthe nature of work prevents the employee from maintaining 3-point contact. \nExemptions: A fall protection system is not required in the US and Canada when: \n • An engineered system is being installed by a qualified and authorized person. \n • A bridge inspector who is trained and qualified in proper climbing techniques \nis engaged in bridge inspection. \n • Working on a guarded structure, unless working within 6 feet of any \nunguarded edge, or if above and beyond the safety of the guardrails. \n • Walking across an unguarded railroad bridge, provided that: \no the employee remains within the outside rails;\no  there are no openings between the outside running rails or bridge ties in \nexcess of 12 inches or through which an employee may fall;\no employees proceed directly over the bridge (no work on the bridge); \no  clear sightlines exist which provide a clear and unobstructed view of \napproaching trains to allow safe traversing of the bridge; and \no  the presence of inclement weather that may impair visibility or walking \nsurface safety does not exist.\n2. Use 3-point contact as a fall protection system only when climbing or descending \na fixed ladder at a height less than 24 feet (7.3 m) (US and Canada) or 20 feet \n(6 m) (Mexico) if it is not equipped with a Lad-Saf™ fall protection system. \n3. Use fall protection systems identified and in accordance with approved \nprocedures in the fall protection plan and manufacturer’s instructions. 42",
      "order": 43,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 44,
        "label": "Page 44"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p45_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n4. Review fall rescue plan/procedures prior to each job requiring the use of fall \narrest equipment. Ensure Personal Floatation Device (PFD)/life rings/rescue boats \nare used where appropriate when working over water. \n5. Inspect full-body harness, anchors and connecting hardware prior to each use \naccording to prescribed instructions and record the results in the Fall Protection \nEquipment Inspection Logbook accompanying your equipment. \n6. Fall protection and pole climbing waist belts are not to be used. \n7. Tools, materials, and equipment must be secured (e.g. guarding, pouches, bags, \nharnesses and lanyards) while working at height. \nLadders\n8. Only use commercially available ladders that meet CSA standards (CAN) / ANSI  \nStandards (US/Mexico).\n9. Inspect ladders prior to and after use to ensure no damage is present. If \ndamage is present advise manager and dispose of ladder. \n10. Use non-conducting type ladders near communication, signal, and electrical wires. \n11. Rest a straight ladder against a firm surface using a 4:1 ratio. Place the ladder on \nfirm footing and secure it in such a manner that it cannot be dislodged accidently \nfrom its position. Take extra precautions if there is a chance the ladder could slip \nout because the ground is slippery such as mud, snow, or ice. \nMethods of securement include but are not limited to:\n • Being tied with straps.\n • Held by another person.\n • Placed on the ground in such a way the ladder will not slip out when climbed.\n • Held in place by the ground itself (i.e. gravel, rocks, sandbags etc.)\n12. Set stepladder on a firm surface, and fully open with four points of contact and \nspreaders fully extended. \n13. Climb no higher than the third rung from the top of a straight ladder or the \nsecond step from the top of a step ladder. \n14. When working in elevated position over track or street, keep rope, wire, chain \ntackle, and other objects from fouling vehicles or trains. \n15. Maintain 3-point contact and face the ladder when climbing or descending  \na ladder. \n16. Do not climb a ladder on which someone else is climbing or descending. \n17. When working on a ladder, maintain two point contact with your feet and keep \nyour center of mass within the beams of the ladder at all times.\nPole Climbing and Line Safety\n18. Only qualified and authorized employees can perform pole climbing and pole \ntop rescue. \n19. Use only properly-sized fall protection equipment for climbing poles. \n43",
      "order": 44,
      "parent_id": null,
