    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p46_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n20. Inspect pole above and below the ground for unsafe conditions before climbing. \nDo not climb until unsafe conditions have been corrected. \n21. Suspend work on line wire during approach of electrical storm or inclement \nweather. \n22. Do not climb a pole while another person is climbing above until that person is \nin position.\nScaffolding and Staging\n23. Erect and dismantle all scaffolding and staging under the direction of a \nqualified and authorized person. \n24. Prior to each use a qualified and authorized person must inspect the scaffolding. \n25. All scaffold systems must be capable of carrying at least four times the safe \nworking load. \n26. If you are the qualified person, ensure that the scaffold system is not \noverloaded. \n27. Ensure walk areas beneath elevated work areas are protected from falling tools \nor materials. \nE-31 Confined Space\nFor more information reference: CPKC Confined Space Procedure \nA confined space: \n1. An enclosed or partially enclosed space that is not designed or intended for \ncontinuous human occupancy except for the purpose of preforming work. \n2. Has limited or restricted means of entry or exit or an internal configuration that \ncould complicate provisions of first aid, evacuation, rescue, or other emergency \nresponse. \n • may be hazardous when entered, occupied, or exited by persons, presents \nhazards likely to cause injury, illness or other adverse health effects to \npersons entering, exiting, or occupying it because of: \no its design, construction, location, or atmosphere;\no the materials or substances in it; or \no any other conditions relating to it. \n3. Confined spaces include but are not limited to tanks, vessels, silos, storage bins, \nhoppers, pits, manholes, equipment housings, ductwork, etc. \n4. A permit required confined space is a confined space that has one or more of \nthe following characteristics or requirements: \n • contains or has the potential to contain a hazardous atmosphere; \n • contains a material that has the potential for engulfing an entrant; \n • has an internal configuration such that an entrant could be trapped \nor asphyxiated by inwardly converging walls or by a floor which slopes \ndownward and tapers to a smaller cross-section; or \n • contains any other recognized serious safety or health hazard (e.g., poor \nillumination, high voltage, noise, lock-out, etc.). 44",
      "order": 45,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 46,
        "label": "Page 46"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p47_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n5. Only qualified and authorized individuals may enter a confined space or \nparticipate in a confined space entry task. \n6. Consider all confined spaces hazardous. Possess a permit and a written rescue \nplan before entering a permit required confined space. \n7. Prior to entry of a permit required confined space if potential for contaminated \natmosphere exists, the atmosphere must be monitored using approved \nequipment. \n8. The atmosphere is required to be monitored and results documented at intervals \nas the work is being performed. \nE-32 Trenching and Excavations \n1. Prior to any digging or excavation you must “call before you dig”, this includes \nCPKC or other public/private, provincial/state underground utility. \nFor CPKC cable locates maintained by S&C on CPKC’s right-of-way call: \nIn Canada call 1-888-248-4410 \nIn the US call 1-866-291-0741 \nFor US only fiber optic cable locates on CPKC right-of-way call: 1-888-625-8702 \nFor non-CPKC cables and utilities on or crossing the CPKC right-of-way, a locate \nrequest must also be made to the appropriate state or provincial agency. \n2. Any person who enters or works in a trench or excavation greater than 4.5 \nfeet/1.4 m deep must be qualified and authorized and protected from cave ins \nby a protective system that prevents collapse of the trench and/or envelopment \nof the employee\n3. Excavation slopes and/or supporting systems must be inspected daily for erosion \nor deterioration. \n4. Any unattended excavation, holes, or trenches on CPKC property shall be \ncovered, guarded and/or protected. \n5. Where an excavation or trench constitutes a hazard to employees or the public, \ninstall a highly visible barricade around it. \n6. Areas within 3ft (1.0 m) of the excavation perimeter must be level and must be \nkept clear of material and equipment. \n7. Hand dig (no mechanical equipment) within 1M/3.3 feet of located S&C cables \nand utilities. \n45",
      "order": 46,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 47,
        "label": "Page 47"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p48_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nAppendix 1: Glossary Terms \nFor the purpose of these rules/work procedures the following terms and  \ndefinitions apply: \nAir: Train’s air brake system, as in “to handle (operate) the air.” \nAir brake hose: The flexible connection between brake pipes. \nAngle cock: The two-position valve located at the end of brake pipes. \nApproved: Acceptable to or meeting CPKC requirements and be obtained from CPKC \napproved lists and/or sources. \nAuthorized: Given the right to act. \nBad order: Equipment that is in need of repair. For example, a defective rail car. \nChemical Safety: Substance which is explosive, flammable, toxic, corrosive, oxidizing, \nirritating, or otherwise harmful to personnel. Includes a controlled product and a \nchemical, biological, or physical agent that is hazardous to the safety or health of the \nperson exposed to it. \nChock: A device used to secure rolling stock when the hand brake is inoperable. \nClamped switch: A clamp applied at the front of the closed switch point to prevent \nmovement of switch points or realignment of switch. \nCommunication Device: Any electronic device capable of communicating remotely, \nthrough oral communications, text messaging, electronic mail, or electronic \ntransmission of any media. This includes but is not limited to: cell phones, walkie-\ntalkies, 2-way radios, PDAs, GPS navigation units, Palm Pilots, Blackberry’s, portable \ncomputers and similar devices. \nCompliance: The act of obeying regulation/law, policy, rule, procedure, or other \nrequirement.\nConfined space: An enclosed or partially enclosed space with a limited or restricted \nentry or exit that is large enough and so configured that a person can enter and \nperform assigned work. A confined space is not designed for continuous human \noccupancy except for the purpose of performing work tasks. \nContractor: A person who in the pursuit of independent business undertakes to \nperform a job or piece of work for CPKC, retaining in himself, control of means, \nmethod and manner of accomplishing the desired result. \nCoupler: An appliance for connecting cars, locomotives, or track units. \nCrossover platform: A horizontal structure on the end of rolling stock to allow a person \nto crossover while stopped. \nDerail: A track safety device designed to guide cars, locomotives or track units off \nthe rails at a selected location as a means of protection against collisions, run-away \nequipment, or other accidents. \nDesignated walkway: An area defined with yellow markings or tape where wearing of \nPPE is not required. \nDesignated platform: A horizontal structure at the end or on the side of a car or \nlocomotive designed for riding on a facing point movement. 46",
      "order": 47,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 48,
        "label": "Page 48"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p49_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nEmployee: Any person in the employ of CPKC, including part and full-time workers but \nnot contractor/vendor workers. \nEmployee in charge: A qualified person appointed by CPKC, through his/her position, \nexperience and knowledge, to ensure the safe and proper conduct of an operation, \ntask or job, or the work of employees. Also, an employee assigned by CPKC to \ncoordinate the work in accordance with the requirements of the safety rules and safe \nwork procedures. \nEOT/SBU device: End of Train or Sense and Braking Unit. A device that defines the end \nof a train and monitors the air brake system and train integrity. \nEquipment: One or more engines and/or cars which can be handled on their own \nwheels in a movement. \nFirm footing: A stance where you are balanced with weight evenly distributed across \nboth feet on secure ground, equipment, or other surface. \nFouling track: The placement of an individual or an item of equipment in such \nproximity to a track that the individual or equipment could be struck by a moving train \nor track unit or in any case within four (4) feet of the field side of the nearest rail. \nFrog: A track structure used at the intersection of two running rails to provide support \nfor wheels and passageways for their flanges, permitting wheels on either rail to cross \nto the other. \nFusee: A red flare for flagging purposes or for raising one’s attention to a hazard or \nemergent condition. \nHand brake: The brake apparatus used to manually apply or release the brakes on \nrolling stock or track unit. \nHandhold: An apparatus affixed to a car, locomotive, track unit, equipment, or \nstructure designed to assist with ascending and descending, or riding. \nHazardous Energy Control-Lockout/Tagout: Methods, devices, and procedures  \nfor preventing the sudden and uncontrolled release of energy from a system, machine, \nor piece of equipment. \nIgnition source: Heat, flame, spark and static electricity that is capable of causing a fuel \nmixture to burn. \nJob briefing: A discussion held among co-workers prior to and during the performance \nof work to review the work to be performed, the associated hazards, control measures, \nemergency procedures, protection measures, and tools/equipment required. \nKnuckle: The pivoting casting that fits into the head of a coupler to engage a mating \ncoupler. \nLadder: Structure consisting of a series of bars or steps between two upright lengths of \nwood, metal or rope used for climbing up or down something. \nMinimum distance from live electrical power line: See chart showing minimum safe \ndistance to power line in Electrical Safety. \nMobile Equipment: Any self-propelled equipment not operating on-track or licensed \nto operate on the road, including forklifts, track mobiles, tractors, cranes, ATVs, Mules, \nand similar equipment. \nOperator: The person who actively controls any motorized equipment. 47",
      "order": 48,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 49,
        "label": "Page 49"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p50_b0",
      "title": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Engineering Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nPersonal Entertainment Device: Any personal radio, TV, DVD player, CD player, game \nplayer, computer, iPod, MP3 player, or similar electronic video or audio entertainment \ndevice which may distract a person from safely performing their work. \nPersonal Protective Equipment (PPE): A device or item of apparel worn to protect  \na worker. \nProper body mechanics: Work activity which follows CPKC’s Movement Safety \nGuidelines. \nProper authority: Those individuals who are qualified by virtue of their expertise \nor their position of leadership to approve, certify or sanction. Also, having secured \napproval for acting in a particular manner. \nQualified and Authorized: A status attained by an employee who has successfully \ncompleted any required training and demonstrated proficiency in the duties of a \nparticular position or function and given the right to act. \nRestricted clearance: Area where the position of any object will not permit the passage \nof a train or other movements on the track, including anyone riding on  \nsuch movements. \nSpiked switch: A spike applied at the front of the closed switch point to prevent \nmovement of switch points or realignment of the switch. \nThermite Welding: The process of igniting a mix of high energy materials, (which is \nalso called thermite), that produce a molten metal that is poured between the working \npieces of metal to form a welded joint. \nThree-point contact: When ascending/descending ladders or stairs, and getting on/off \nvehicles or equipment contact must consist of two hands and one foot, or two feet and \none hand. \nThree-point protection: A procedure used to protect employees when fouling \nequipment. This procedure requires both the employee being protected and the \nemployee providing the protection to act together when providing and releasing the \nprotection. \nTrack Unit: A vehicle or machine capable of on-track operation utilized for track \ninspection, track work and other railway activities when on a track. \nVehicle: Any owned, leased, rented or private vehicle licensed to operate on public \nroads when used for company business by any CPKC employee, contractor or other  \n3rd party authorized to be on CPKC property. \nWheel chock: A device, when placed firmly against both sides of a rail car, locomotive, \nor track unit wheel, prevents it from rolling unintentionally. \n48",
      "order": 49,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 50,
        "label": "Page 50"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_eng_safety_rule_book_april_2023_english_p51_b0",
