    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p6_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nOrganizing the Information\nThe Train & Engine Safety Rule Book is organized into two main sections:\nSection I:\nCore Safety Rules: These are common rules to all functions and must be complied with \nand enforced at all times. \nSection II:\nSafety Rules: These are function specific rules that must be complied with and enforced \nat all times. They are written to address the work of Train & Engine employees. \nSafe Work Procedures: These procedures outline the steps to safely perform the task. \nSafe work procedures must be complied with at all times. \nPersonal Protective Equipment (PPE) Chart: This chart has been created to assist  \nthe user in determining the appropriate PPE that is required based on the task to  \nbe performed. \n4",
      "order": 5,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 6,
        "label": "Page 6"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p7_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nCORE Safety Rules\n1� Rights and Responsibilities\nAll employees must make certain that:\n • Job tasks are performed only by individuals who are qualified and authorized. \n • Job briefings/task assessments are conducted before performing work and when \nactivity and/or conditions change. \n • We advise and redirect to safe work practices anyone observed performing an \nunsafe act. \n • We immediately inform each other of unsafe conditions, correct/protect it and \nreport it to a supervisor. \n • Accidents, incidents, including injuries, and related information are immediately \nreported to a supervisor. \n • Warning signs, posted instructions, placards, and/or barriers marking restricted \nareas and lockout tagout areas are displayed and complied with at all times. \n • We do not participate in horseplay or physical altercations while on Company \nproperty or participating in Company activities. \n • One person does not engage in work activity that by legislation or Company \npolicy requires two or more people. \n • A copy of the function specific safety rules must be accessible while on duty. \n • We do not possess or use a firearm on Company property unless authorized by  \nCPKC Police Services. \n2� Substance Abuse\n • Inappropriate substance use is prohibited as identified in CPKC Drug &  \nAlcohol Policies . \n • Do not smoke/use electronic cigarettes in company buildings, vehicles,  \nand machines.\n • For more information see CPKC HR Policy. \n3� Non-CPKC Personnel \nEmployees responsible for non-CPKC personnel must:\n • Understand the terms of the Minimum Safety Requirements for Contractors \nworking on CPKC property, follow access control procedures, and ensure they; \no  Receive a site-specific safety orientation and job briefing.\no  Wear PPE according to location-specific requirements. \no  Do not foul track without proper protection.\no  Have a Contractor Site Safety Plan/Emergency Information Sheet. \no  Have appropriate identification. \n4� Emergencies and Derailments\n • Follow all notification procedures as outlined in location-specific Emergency \nResponse Plans. \n • Approach an emergency or a derailment site only when safe to do so. \n5",
      "order": 6,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 7,
        "label": "Page 7"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p8_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n • When arriving at an emergency site, contact the On-Scene Response Coordinator \nor Person-In-Charge for a job briefing and follow all procedures outlined in the \njob briefing. \n • Following an accident or incident, immediately notify a supervisor/dispatcher and \ndo not contact human remains, blood, or other fluids.\n5� Fire Safety\n • Maintain unrestricted access to, and be familiar with the location of, fire \nextinguishers, alarm boxes, exit aisles, circuit breakers, fuse panels, emergency \nexits and local emergency response plans.\n • Immediately replace or report any defective, discharged or missing fire \nextinguishers. \n • Store flammable and combustible material away from ignition sources.\n • When qualified, use proper grounding and bonding techniques to prevent static \nelectrical charge when dispensing or transferring flammable liquids. \n • Do not leave open flames unattended. \n • Dispose of flammable or combustible materials according to established \nCompany guidelines.\n • Upon discovery of a fire, if safe to do so, extinguish the fire. Employees must \nimmediately activate the fire alarm (where available), and promptly report the \nfire to supervisor, dispatcher and/or proper authority.\n6� Work Environment\n • Keep all work areas clean, orderly, and free from clutter and debris. \n • Must properly dispose of refuse in appropriate and approved receptacle or \nwaste bin. \n • Employees who use needles, lancets and medical sharps must properly dispose of \nthem in sharps containers, if provided, or remove them from company property \nfor disposal, if not provided.  Do not throw needles, lancets, and medical sharps \nin company trash.\n • Use approved products to wash equipment, clothing, or floors. \n • Do not use compressed air or gases to remove debris from body or clothing. \n • Do not walk under suspended loads. \n • Work materials must be kept neatly and not stored above shoulder height unless \nthe area is designed for palletized equipment/materials with a specified load \nlimit and a forklift is utilized.\n • Walkways must be kept clear to eliminate tripping hazards. \n7� Vehicles, Materials, Equipment, and Tools\n • Operate, maintain, and repair vehicles, equipment, tools, and materials only \nwhen qualified, properly licensed and authorized. \n • Use CSA B376 or equivalent UL / ULC (CAN) or Department of Transportation \n(DOT) (US/Mexico) approved, properly marked containers when storing or \ntransporting flammable liquids or materials.\n • Use approved tools, equipment, and materials for the purpose(s) intended. \n • Do not modify or override safety devices or remove safety guards. \n6",
      "order": 7,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 8,
        "label": "Page 8"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p9_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\n • Maintain, repair, and store equipment according to prescribed instructions. \n • Inspect all vehicles, equipment, tools, and related safety devices for unsafe \nconditions before use. \n • Repair or tag and remove from service if defective. \n • Use three-point contact when getting on or off any equipment, machinery, \nladder, or vehicle. \n • Face the vehicle or equipment/track unit when getting on or off. \n • Ventilate exhaust when operating internal-combustion equipment inside \nbuildings or enclosed structures. \n8. Office Safety\n • When sitting in a chair, keep all chair legs or casters on the floor. \n • When not in use, chairs shall be kept pushed in to keep walkways and \nworkspaces clear. \n • Roller style chairs are only allowed at workstations, training, and  \nmeeting/office rooms. \n • Fixed leg chairs are required at ready room and lunch tables. \n • Use only commercially available stepstools that meet CSA standards (CAN)/ANSI \nStandards (US/Mexico) to access elevated locations.\n • Ensure that desk and file cabinet doors and drawers are closed when not in use. \n • When ascending or descending flights of stairs employees must utilize handrails. \n9� Use of Electronic Devices\nFor Employees governed by GCOR/OTS or CROR refer to the applicable operating rules \nand related system special instructions� \nFor employees not governed by GCOR/OTS or CROR refer to the CPKC Use of Electronic \nDevices Procedure for applicable instructions. \n7",
      "order": 8,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 9,
        "label": "Page 9"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p10_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nTrain & Engine Safety Rules and Safe Work Procedures\nT-0 Job Briefing\n1. Before performing any job, a job briefing led by the foreman/conductor must be \nheld to ensure that all employees involved have a clear understanding of: \n • The task to be performed; \n • Your individual responsibility; and \n • Situational awareness concerns\nAdditional verbal job briefings must be carried out as necessary, while the work \nprogresses or as the situation changes. \nSafe Work Procedure: Job Briefings\nCrew members must ensure they are aware of conditions that will affect the safe \noperation of movements. Employees must: \n • Ensure all operating bulletins and notices are read and understood; \n • Obtain, read and discuss operating authorities as required; \n • Confirm proper designation of movement on TGBO and all operating authorities; \n • Identify presence of dimensional, speed restrictions and special dangerous cars; \n • Ensure proper documentation for all dangerous goods/hazardous materials; \n • Discuss and review items affecting the territory the movement will operate  \nover; and \n • Obtain permission to depart and confirm routing and limits of authority, \nincluding location of first restriction. \nTo ensure all crew members maintain awareness of conditions that will affect the safe \noperation of the movement enroute they must verbally discuss between themselves: \n • Environmental and crew fatigue assessments; \n • Fixed signals encountered; \n • Operating restrictions en-route; \n • Set-off / Set-out and lifts/pick-ups prior to reaching work location and ensure \nproper marshalling; \n • Proper train speed; \n • Documentation; \n • Transfer requirements if relieved enroute; and \n • Proper securement of cars set out. \nAt the final terminal or end of shift ensure crew members:\n • Discuss arrival and yarding instructions; \n • Ensure proper securement of the movement; \n • Report any equipment car or locomotive defects or any needed supplies; and \n • Confirm documentation and details of enroute and supplemental work \nperformed is reported.\n8",
      "order": 9,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 10,
        "label": "Page 10"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p11_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nT-1 Air Hoses, Handling\n1. Reaching over a drawbar to open an angle cock is \nprohibited. \n2. You may reach over a drawbar to close an angle cock \nprovided the angle cock can be reached without leaning \non the drawbar or coupler and no part of your body \nbecomes exposed to any pinch point. \n3. Turn your head away from the glad hands when air \nhoses are being uncoupled. \n4. Never kick, strike at, or jostle the air hose(s) to stop a leak. \n5. Treat all air hoses and angle cocks as if they are under \npressure. \n6. When separating equipment with a charged brake pipe: \n • close the angle cock on the equipment to be moved; \n • leave the angle cock open on the standing equipment; \n • lift the operating lever to release pin; \n • separate the equipment; and \n • observe and communicate to your crew that the equipment left standing  \nis in emergency. \nSafe Work Procedure: Coupling Air Hoses\nStep 1:   Ensure equipment is secure, if equipment is attached to a locomotive, ensure \n3-point protection is in place. \nStep 2:  Step between the cars. Keeping one foot outside the rail whenever possible. \nStep 3:  Make sure that both glad hands have gaskets. \nStep 4:   Bend at the knees and hips while maintaining a neutral spine (i.e. keeping \nyour lower back, mid-back and neck curves in proper alignment) grasp the  \nair hose nearest you firmly behind its glad hand and bend it upward.\nStep 5:  Grasp the hose farthest away and pull it toward the bent hose. \nStep 6:   Match the glad hands into opposite contoured slots and push them \ndownward. \nStep 7:  Make certain that the glad hands are seated. \nStep 8:  With your head turned away, gradually open the angle cock. \nSafe Work Procedure: Opening Angle Cocks\nStep 1:  Ensure 3-point protection is in place. \nStep 2:   With your head turned away, gradually open angle cock, bracing hose \nagainst leg. \nStep 3:   If you hear an air leak, close both angle cocks and make sure there is no \npressure in the hoses before adjusting or repairing the leak. \n9",
      "order": 10,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 11,
        "label": "Page 11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p12_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nSafe Work Procedure: Opening an angle cock or “dumping the air” \nto an uncoupled air hose\nStep 1:  Ensure 3-point protection is in place. \nStep 2: Grasp the hose at the glad hand clear of the vent port. \nStep 3: Brace the glad hand firmly against your thigh just above the knee. \nStep 4:   Turn your face away from the glad hand before slowly opening the angle cock. \nSafe Work Procedure: Uncoupling Air Hoses\nStep 1:  Ensure 3-point protection is in place. \nStep 2:  Close both angle cocks. \nStep 3:  Crouching down, firmly grasp each glad hand with your hand near the coupling. \nStep 4:   With your arm extended and with a quick motion, pull upward on the  \nglad hands. \nT-4 Vehicles Used for Company Business\n1. Inspect vehicles for unsafe conditions before use. Repair or tag and remove from \nservice if defective. \n2. Prior to operation of a vehicle the driver must conduct a walk around of the \nvehicle to identify any obstacles, clearance restrictions, or adjacent vehicles that \nmay interfere with executing a safe movement. When backing up use a spotter \nif one is readily available.\n3. Headlights/daytime running lights must be on when operating a vehicle.\n4. Wear seat belt while operating or riding in motor vehicles equipped with  \nthem, unless engaged in inspections and traveling less than 15 MPH (25 KPH)  \non CPKC property.\n5. While being transported in vehicles, ride only in seats permanently installed or \nin approved, identified riding locations. \n6. Do not use hand held cell phones while operating a company vehicle or mobile \nequipment while it is in motion on any type of road except in an emergency. \n7. Use company provided cell phones, hands free only, for limited use while \noperating a company vehicle or mobile equipment if: \n • It is solely for company business; \n • It is safe to do so; and \n • The vehicle or mobile equipment being operated is NOT on a public roadway  \nin a province, state, or other jurisdiction where such use is in violation of any  \nlaw or regulation. \n8. Do not get on or off a moving vehicle. \n9. While transporting tools, materials and equipment with a company vehicle on \nroad or track, loads must be contained, immobilized or secured. \n10. Do not park foul of the track, unless track protection is provided and only  \nwhen necessary. 10",
      "order": 11,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 12,
        "label": "Page 12"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p13_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nT-5 Aligning Drawbar / Coupler\n1. Separate equipment by a minimum of 50 feet. \n2. Ensure that equipment is secured and 3-point protection is provided. \n3. Do not adjust coupler by kicking it with your foot. \n4. Do not lift a drawbar. \nSafe Work Procedure: Aligning Coupler with Backwards or Forward \nFacing Method \nStep 1:  Ensure three point protection is in place and/or cars \nare secured with a minimum of 50 feet separation.\nStep 2: Stand to the side of the coupler.\nStep 3:  Face or turn your back to the coupler leaning \nagainst it.\nStep 4:  Put one foot beneath the coupler, one foot in front \nof you or one foot behind you, depending on the \nmethod.\nStep 5:  Watch your footing, especially during wet and/or \nwinter conditions. Do not step on the rail when \nwalking; however, it is permitted to brace your \nfoot on the rail for added leverage when aligning \nthe drawbar using this technique.\nStep 6:  Grip the coupler from beneath and watch your \nfingers.\nStep 7: Keep the load close to your body.\nStep 8:  Bend at the knees and hips while maintaining a  \nneutral spine.\nStep 9:  Raise up from the knees, using your leg muscles, \nand shift your weight forwards or backwards to \npush the drawbar over. Do not lift the drawbar or elevate your shoulders \nwhen pushing.\nStep 10:  Use whole body movements, pushing small distances, stopping and \nrepositioning as needed (repeating the steps above) until the drawbar is \naligned. Do not over exert.\nT-6 Coupling / Uncoupling\n1. Before coupling cars:\n • If riding equipment prior to coupling, detrain (if riding a locomotive, you are \nexempt from detraining); and \n • Ensure couplers are aligned and that at least one knuckle is open. \n2. Only use an operating lever under the following conditions:\n • while stopped \n • at a walking pace facing the direction of travel, never run; or \n11",
      "order": 12,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 13,
        "label": "Page 13"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p14_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n • On the locomotive, while riding in the locomotive stairwell \n • Use only your hand to operate the operating lever.\n3. If required to adjust the knuckle by hand:\n • separate equipment by a minimum of 50 feet; \n • ensure 3-point protection is provided, confirm the slack has adjusted and no \nmovement will occur on the unattached equipment; and \n • with one foot outside of the rail, lift the operating lever with your left hand, \ngrab the middle of the knuckle with your right hand and pull the knuckle open. \nT-7 Knuckle Replacement\n1. When removing or replacing knuckles, keep feet clear of area directly  \nbeneath knuckle. \n2. Do not insert your fingers through the hole at the bottom of a coupler to raise \nthe lock block to open the knuckle or any pinch points inside the coupler. \nSafe Work Procedure: Replacing Knuckles\nStep 1:   Ensure 3-point protection is in place or cars are secured with a minimum of \n50 ft separation. \nStep 2:   Obtain the same knuckle type as removed.\nStep 3:   Keeping feet clear of the area under the coupler, make sure the knuckle pin \nis in place and open the knuckle. \nStep 4:  Remove the cotter pin and then the knuckle pin. \nStep 5:  Remove the knuckle from the coupler. \nStep 6:  Holding the old knuckle close to the body (with stable base of support) place \nit away from the immediate work area so it will not become a tripping or \noperational hazard.\nStep 7: Drop the replacement knuckle over the rail first before repeating the squat lift.\nStep 8:  Move the lock block back into the coupler recess with the operating lever \nheld up with an operating lever strap or other available item (i.e. stick, belt).\nStep 9:  Use a squat technique, with wide stance, bending through the hips and \nknees, keeping your back neutral as you lift the knuckle off the ground and \nplace it into the coupler pocket. \nStep 10: Close the knuckle.\nStep 11:  Insert the knuckle pin into the pinhole and cotter pin, with both hands free, \nmaking sure it locks properly. \nT-8 Crossing Over or Between Equipment\n1. Use only the appropriate safety appliances when \ncrossing over equipment (crossover platforms, \nladders, handholds, locomotive platforms) and \nmaintain three points of contact at all times. \nException: Employees may cross over intermodal 12",
      "order": 13,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 14,
        "label": "Page 14"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p15_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\ncars not equipped with continuous handholds, provided the walking platform \nis wide enough to safely cross over.  \nBefore crossing over ensure the car is secured and will not be moved.\n2. It is permissible to cross from one car to another car provided all of the \nfollowing are met; \n • Cars are coupled and stationary \n • Three points of contact are maintained at all times \n • Both cars have appropriate end crossover platforms and grab irons\n3. The following is prohibited, do not cross:\n • under equipment. \n • over or between coupled, moving cars; or \n • over or between cars without end platforms (e.g. multi-level autos). \n4. Do not step onto the coupler, striker casting, sliding center sill, coupler shank, \nangle cock, air hose or trainline, journal box, operating lever, or truck side.\nT-9 Derails\n1. Prior to operating a derail: \n • ensure the track is either clear of traffic in both directions or equipment is \nsecured to prevent uncontrolled movement and; \n • ensure that your movement is stopped 100 feet from an applied manual \nderail. \n2. If a lock is present, lock derails in non-derailing position when switching. \n3. Test a flip-type derail (with or without handle) for resistance before attempting \nto lift. \nT-11 Entraining and Detraining Equipment\n1. When conditions are determined to be safe, and only after the locomotive \nengineer confirms the speed of the movement is 4 MPH or less, employees are \npermitted to entrain or detrain moving equipment. \n2. Always verbally communicate the intent to the locomotive engineer (includes \nRCLS / RCO operator) prior to entraining or detraining moving equipment. The \nlocomotive engineer must verbally acknowledge the intention of entraining \nor detraining the movement and confirm to the employee when the speed is \n4 MPH or less at the entraining or detraining location. \n3. Always verbally communicate to the locomotive engineer once you have safely \nentrained or detrained moving equipment. \n4. Never entrain or detrain moving equipment while in possession of a grip/bag or \nany item that would prevent the full use of both hands (Eg. EOT’s, tools, water, \nswitch broom).\n5. Entrain and detrain clear of switch stands, bridge approaches, retaining walls, \nrestricted / close clearances, debris and other fixed objects. \n13",
      "order": 14,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 15,
        "label": "Page 15"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p16_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n6. Ensure your body is facing the stairwell or side ladder on equipment when \nentraining or detraining.\n7. Do not jump from any piece of equipment or structure to ground level or onto \nanother adjacent equipment or structure except in an Emergency situation. \n8. Use 3 points of contact on steps, ladders, railings, or handrails when entraining \nor detraining any piece of equipment or structure, maintaining a firm grip. \n9. Never entrain or detrain moving equipment if the car is a flat car not equipped \nwith handholds extending at least 18 inches above the deck of the car.\n10. Entrain or Detrain moving equipment on the leading end of equipment. \nException – It is permissible to Entrain or Detrain the trailing end of a car or \nlocomotive only when it is the last piece of equipment of a movement.\n11. Always look both directions and for stable footing when getting on or off \nequipment.\nSafe Work Procedure: Entraining moving equipment \nStep 1:  Communicate the intention to entrain to the locomotive engineer/controlling \noperator and receive confirmation from the locomotive engineer/controlling \noperator that the speed is 4 MPH or less prior to entraining moving \nequipment. Stand facing the approaching movement, clear of its path. \nStep 2:   Ensure that your entraining area: \n • Will provide solid footing, and \n • Does not have any object or condition that will cause you to slip or trip. \nStep 3:  Do not attempt to entrain if you think the equipment is moving too fast, \nreduce speed to a safe level. \nStep 4:  Grasp handrails with both hands and, in sync with the movement, step into \nthe stirrup with the trailing foot (relative to the direction of movement) first \nand then the other foot. \nStep 5:  Communicate to the locomotive engineer/controlling operator once you have  \nsafely entrained. \nSafe Work Procedure: Detraining moving equipment\nStep 1:  Communicate your intention to detrain to the locomotive engineer/\ncontrolling operator receive confirmation from the locomotive engineer/\ncontrolling operator that the speed is 4 MPH or less prior to detraining \nmoving equipment. When getting off equipment, always face the direction \nof travel. Visually select a safe area to detrain well in advance. \nStep 2:  Ensure that your detraining area: \n • Will provide solid footing, and \n • Does not have any object or condition that will cause you to slip or trip. \nStep 3:  When getting off equipment, always face the equipment that you are about \nto detrain and look where you are going to place your feet. \n14",
      "order": 15,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 16,
        "label": "Page 16"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p17_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nStep 4:  As you get closer: \n • Narrow your focus on a detraining spot, checking that it is free of tripping \nhazards. \n • Drop your trailing foot (relative to the direction of movement) from the \nstirrup. \n • Lower your trailing foot to the ground, lining your toes in the direction of \nmovement and step away with the leading foot releasing your lead hand. \nStep 5:  Maintain a grip on the handhold with your trailing hand (relative to the \ndirection of movement) until you are balanced on your feet. \nStep 6:  Once your balance is ensured, release your trailing hand from the handhold \nand step away from the track. \nStep 7:  Communicate to the locomotive engineer once you are safely off the \nequipment. \nT-13 Fusees\n1. Store fusees in authorized locations, such as approved flagging kits.\n2. When igniting a fusee:\n • hold and strike it away from your body;\n • do not look directly at the fusee; and\n • do not ignite near fuel sources and be on guard for sparks.\n3. When extinguishing the fusee:\n • Bury the burning end in sand or loose dirt \n • Gently strike the burning end of the fusee over the edge of a rail or a heavy \nmetal object to ensure the burning compound is separated from the rest of \nthe fusee; or \n • Let the fusee burn to completion. \n4. After extinguishing a fusee, dispose of it in an appropriate place. Do not allow \nthe burning compound to come in contact with combustible material. \nSafe Work Procedure: Fusees\nStep 1: Hold the fusee near the base.\nStep 2:  Pull the tape over the top to expose the scratch surface on the end of the \ncap.\nStep 3:  Twist the cap away from the head of the fusee.\nStep 4:  Hold the cap stationary, turn your face away, and rub the igniter on the head \nof the fusee lightly against the scratch surface of the cap in a motion away \nfrom the body.\nStep 5:  If the fusee does not light, pause before attempting to light it again. While \npausing, keep the fusee pointed away from your face and body.\n15",
      "order": 16,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 17,
        "label": "Page 17"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p18_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nT-14 Hand Brakes\n1. Fully release all hand brakes prior to moving \nequipment, unless cabling a car or applying \nGCOR 7.12. \n2. When operating wheel-type hand brakes, always \ngrip the brake wheel with your thumb on the \noutside of the wheel rim and never overexert. \n3. Do not apply or release wheel style hand brakes \nfrom the ground unless the bottom of the \nhandbrake wheel is at shoulder height or below. \n4. When releasing hand brakes check at least \ntwo cars beyond the last hand brake found to \nensure there are no more hand brakes applied. \n5. It is permissible to apply a hand brake for the purpose of controlling free rolling \nequipment, the handbrake must only be applied with sufficient force not to cause \nthe wheels to slide or lock up. \n6. Never use both hands or a foot to operate a hand brake. Exception: Both \nhands may be used to operate a staff-type brake and a foot may be used to \noperate the pawl.\n7. Where authorized by SVP Operations, Company approved brake sticks may be \nused to operate vertical wheel style hand brakes, retainer valves, open knuckles, \nangle cocks and press the reset button on an EOT on stationary equipment. The \nuse of defective brake sticks is prohibited. \nWhen using a brake stick to operate a handbrake:\n • If practicable, work from the Field side rather than between adjacent tracks. \n • If working between adjacent tracks keep clear of moving equipment. \n • Crew members must comply with the requirements of T-27 3-point protection. \n • Place the hook to the outside of the wheel to avoid the hook from being \ncaught between the wheel and the equipment. \n • Never place the butt of the brake stick against your body, keep it at the side \nof your body. \n • Never cross equipment with the brake stick in hand, pass it through \nequipment \n • Never walk backwards \n • Do not place the brake stick: \no on or remove from moving equipment \no in a location where it may be an obstruction or tripping hazard \n • Do not use the brake stick to:\no Operate defective hand brake wheels\no Operate quick release handles\n8. Keep your body and hands free of all moving parts eg. chain, wheel, release \nhandle. \n9. Never use hand brake wheel as a handhold.\n16\nWhite line indicates where the bot-\ntom of the handbrake wheel should \nbe in relation to the shoulder.",
      "order": 17,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 18,
        "label": "Page 18"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p19_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nT-19 On or About Locomotives\n1. Use door handles or other opening/closing devices to open /close doors or \nwindows whenever provided. \n2. Keep locomotive cab and walkways clean, free of debris, clutter and slip/trip \nhazards. \n3. Ask for assistance when placing grips, bags, MU cables, tools, EOT’s etc. on \nlocomotives, if required. \n4. When running the units through flange ways that may be obstructed with \nsnow, ice or other material. You should detrain and walk clear of the movement \nwatching for wheel climb until you are beyond the condition. \n5. Store tools, EOT’s and MU cables in the designated locations.\nT-20 On or About Tracks\n1. Do not sit or lean against rail equipment. \n2. Look in both directions before:\n • Fouling or crossing any track \n • Getting on or off equipment; or \n • Operating a switch. \n3. Never walk or stand foul of a track between the rails or on the shoulder tie \nends, unless there are no other viable options available and it is safe to do so. \n4. When walking between standing equipment, ensure there is a minimum of 50 \nfeet of separation between equipment. \n5. Allow at least 15 feet when passing around the end of standing equipment \nunless proper protection is provided. \n6. Maintain a safe distance from moving equipment to avoid protruding objects. \n7. Do not step on any part of: \n • a rail; \n • a switch or switch machine/ heater except to operate foot pedal; \n • a frog; \n • a derail; \n • a retarder; \n • a defect detector/hot box detector; \n • Automatic Equipment Identification (AEI) reader; or \n • Wayside interface unit (WIU). \n8. At night and during periods of reduced visibility, use a lantern or approved light \nsource to perform work. \n • All T&E employees MUST use a CPKC supplied hand lantern as an approved \nlight source to perform work. \n • It is permissible for all T&E employees to use a headlamp in addition to the \nCPKC supplied hand held lantern to perform work. \n17",
      "order": 18,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 19,
        "label": "Page 19"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p20_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\n9. Lanterns must always be carried by hand or through your arm and never clipped \nto your body. \nException:\n • Locomotive engineers may use a hand held flashlight while performing the \nduties of a locomotive engineer. \n10. When traversing a bridge be aware of weather conditions, lighting conditions \nand bridge surface conditions. Walk between the rails if no walkway is provided.  \n11. If found in a situation where you are located between adjacent track \nmovements, kneel down and stop the movement you are controlling. \n12. When working at or near crossings, train crews must avoid using hand signals \nfor train operations, which could be misunderstood by motorists using the \ncrossing. \n13. Before going between or working on the end of rail equipment that does not \nhave a locomotive coupled to it: \n • a crew member, must verbally inform other crew members, and other crews \nworking in close proximity by radio and receive acknowledgment from those \nnotified. \n • must not be coupled to and/or handbrakes released that could affect the \nmovement of such equipment without permission from the crew member \nthat is between or at the end of rail equipment. \n14. When duties require you to cross a track, walk directly across the track at a right \nangle (90-degrees). Employees must look both ways prior to each track and \napply a 90-Degree principle when conditions permit. \nBest Practice: \nWhen walking ahead of a movement, ensure you check the position of the movement \noften and never foul the track. \nT-21 Personal Protective Equipment\n1. Use, wear and maintain personal protective equipment and clothing obtained \nfrom approved sources required by job task and/or work environment (including \noff-site industries) as per standard, including the following requirements: \nNote: All PPE below must be readily available when participating in classroom \ntraining\nSafety eyewear must:\n • meet CSA Z94.3 (in Canada) and ANSI Z87.1 (in the US and Mexico);\n • have permanently attached side shields;\n • not contain transition lenses; and\n • not have mirrored finishes\n18",
      "order": 19,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 20,
        "label": "Page 20"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p21_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nSafety Footwear used must follow procedure outlined H&S 5350:\n • meet CSA Z195 (in Canada) and ASTM F13 (in the US and Mexico);\n • have puncture resistant soles (in Canada);\n • have an 8 inches upper as defined by the manufacturer;\n • have a minimum 3/8 inch defined heel;\n • be laced fully to the top,\n • be maintained free of tears and have functioning tread, and\n • be anti-slip when existing snow and/or ice conditions warrant\n • be worn at all times while on duty including deadheading (in taxi)\nHigh Visibility apparel must:\n • meet CSA Z96 Class 2 Level 2 (in Canada) \n • meet CSA Z96 Class 2 Level 2 or ANSI/ISEA - 107 Class 2 (in the US and Mexico) \n • be visible at all times; \n • be worn zippered or snapped up at all times; and \n • be lime green/yellow in color when worn by new hires throughout their first \nyear of service.\nProtective gloves must:\n • Be worn where potential to chemical or physical injury to the hands exists \n(including operating switches, derails, tasks involving cars or locomotive).\nSee PPE and Clothing Chart\n2. Confirm that personal protective equipment is in working condition and not \nmodified before use, removing from service if defective.\n3. Wear ankle-length pants and waist-length shirts with minimum quarter-length \nsleeves at all times.\n4. Clothing must not interfere with vision, hearing or use of hands and feet. It \nmust fit appropriately and/or be secured to prevent catching in machinery or on \nequipment.\n5. Long hair must be tied back or secured.\n6. Jewelry must not be worn around sources of entanglement or electrical \nequipment.\n19",
      "order": 20,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 21,
        "label": "Page 21"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p22_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nT-22 Personal Protective Equipment Chart\nThe following chart indicates minimum PPE requirements for Train & Engine employees  \nwhile conducting.\nT-23 Restricted / Close Clearances\n1. Review the applicable operating bulletins/notices, timetables (special \ninstructions) and job aids for close/restricted clearances during job briefings and \nfollow-up job briefings.\n2. When riding on equipment, detrain before reaching a restricted/close clearance.\n3. Extreme caution must be used if switching movements are required within a \nrestricted/close clearance area. These movements should be no more than a car \nlength in distance and the crew member must place themself in a position that \nallows the maximum margin of safety.\n4. If unable to identify the clearance point when leaving equipment in a track, \nstand outside the rail of the adjacent track and extend the arm towards the \nequipment. When you are unable to touch the equipment, leave the equipment \nat least an additional 50 feet into the track to ensure equipment is beyond the \nclearance point.\nTransportation/Field\nOperations PPE  \nRequirements \nM = Mandatory \nR = As Required\n- = Not Required\nHearing \nProtection\nGloves/ \nMitts\nHigh \nVisibility \nVest\nHard \nHat\nRespiratory \nProtection\nEye  \nProtection\nSafety  \nFootwear\nCrews of work trains R M M R R M M\nCustomer Property R M M R R M M\nHandling switches \ncritical parts on cars, \nengines, EOT/SBU \ndevices\nR M M - - M M\nIn locomotives - closed \nwindows R - - - - - M\nIn locomotives - open \nwindows M - - - - M M\nIntermodal Facilities R M M R - M M\nRiding on locomotives \noutside of cab M M M - - M M\nRiding on rail cars - M M - - M M\nTrain inspections R R M R - M M\nWorking auxiliaries/ \nderailments R M M M R M M\nWorking in\nclose proximity to \nretarders R - M - - M M\nNote:  Local management may designate other areas or jobs that require additional PPE. All PPE must meet \nCPKC approved standards.\n20",
      "order": 21,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 22,
        "label": "Page 22"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p23_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nT-24 Riding Equipment\n1. Use the side ladder of the lead end of equipment \non the engineers side whenever possible and \nalways observe the direction of travel. Whenever \npossible ride on the engineers side. \n2. It is permissible to ride the platform of a \nlocomotive, or other equipment with a designated \nriding platform (e.g. passenger cars).\n3. It is permissible to ride the platform of the leading \nend of a tank car with the employees feet outside of \nthe rail and body positioned outside of the handrail.\n4. It is permissible to ride the end platform of a car \nonly on the trailing end of a movement.\n5. It is prohibited to move from one car to another car while moving. (unless \nequipment is designed for movement between cars i.e. locomotives, passenger \ncars, rail grinding train, etc.)\n6. Always maintain at least 3 points of contact using foot, crook of arm or hands. \nWhen riding equipment, use only designated handholds and ensure your feet \nare facing the stirrup, ladder or stairwell, prepared for slack action.\n7. The following is prohibited, do not ride:\n • the lead end platform or front ladder of equipment except as noted in \n#3 above or when operating a handbrake on cars rolling under their own \nmomentum;\n • on the deck of a flat car, bulkhead or centerbeam;\n • a flat car not equipped with handholds extending at least 18 inches above \nthe deck of the car;\n • middle ladder on a tank car;\n • on any equipment while entering, inside, or leaving any building or structure;\n • on the roof of any rolling stock or on the lading of any car;\n • inside the end cage of equipment;\n • inside a gondola; or\n • between cars unless operating a handbrake on cars rolling under their own \nmomentum.\n8. It is prohibited to ride the bottom step of equipment over public crossings at grade.\n9. When conditions permit ride on the opposite side of an adjacent track where \nequipment is present.\n10. Ride the side ladder of equipment, only if hand holds and stirrup configuration \nallow for a firm grip and an erect and normal body position. Never lean your \nupper body back from the side ladder unless it is safe to do so. Ensure there are \nno switch stands, equipment in adjacent tracks, or other clearance issues. \n11. Always advise the locomotive engineer or controlling operator that you are \nriding equipment.\n21",
      "order": 22,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 23,
        "label": "Page 23"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p24_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nT-26 Switches\n1. Keep your body, hands, and feet clear of all moving parts and out of the path of \nthe switch handle. \n2. Do not apply force with your foot on a switch/derail handle. \n3. Prior to operating switches, check the switch rods and switch points for ice, \nballast, or any other debris that may prevent the switch from lining freely. Check \nif the switch has been spiked. \n4. Remove the switch point lock pedal, where equipped, before operating switch. \n5. Ensure switch points fit properly prior to allowing a movement to pass. \n6. Reapply the switch lock or keeper after the switch has been lined. \n7. Never use excessive force while operating a switch. \n8. Use both hands while operating the switch handle to align the switch points  \nor derail.\nSafe Work Procedure: Cleaning Switches - Winter Months\nStep 1:  Using switch broom, clean area between the switch points and the heel blocks.\nStep 2:  When cleaning the switch points, ensure that they are free of ice and hard-\npacked snow. (The switch points glide on the switch plates.) \nStep 3:  Start with a wide stance to create a stable base of support. Position your \nfeet so your hips and shoulders face the work to minimize twisting and side \nbending. Bend at the hips and knees, with your back in neutral while you \nsweep the brush across your body. Use short strokes and move your feet to \navoid twisting or overreaching.\nStep 4:  To pick at any ice, use the scraper tool on the opposite end of the handle and \nbend deeper through the hips and knees to get close to the work (without \nbending through the back). \nStep 5: Clean connecting rods.\nStep 6:  Ensure switch points are thoroughly and properly cleaned and there is no \ngap between the switch point and stock rail. \n22",
      "order": 23,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 24,
        "label": "Page 24"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p25_b0",
      "title": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC",
      "text": "April 2023 Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC\nT-27 3-Point Protection\n1. Provide 3-point protection if cars are \nconnected to locomotives and working on the \nground when: \n • one or both feet enter between the rail, \nbetween equipment or within 15 feet of the \nend of the equipment; or \n • breaking the plane of the rail with your \ntorso, between equipment or within 15 feet \nof the end of equipment. \n2. When applying 3-point protection the \nautomatic brake is not required if the engineer \nascertains that the locomotive brake(s) \nare sufficient to hold the equipment. The \nemployee requesting the 3-point protection \ncan still request that the automatic brake \nbe applied if needed to prevent unintended \nmovement. \n3. Any other department i.e. mechanical, \nengineering etc., that requests 3-point \nprotection must have the automatic brake \napplied if applicable. \n4. When more than one employee requests 3-point protection, ensure that each \nemployee cancels the requested protection before removing. \nSafe Work Procedure: Applying 3-Point Protection\nOnce a request for 3-point protection is received:\nStep 1:  Apply engine brakes and automatic brake (if required) \nStep 2:  Center the reverser \nStep 3:  Open the generator field switch \nStep 4:   Communicate to your crew member that 3- point protection has  \nbeen applied. \nSafe Work Procedure: Releasing 3-Point Protection\nOnce confirmation is received that 3-point protection is to be removed:\nStep 1:  Close the generator field switch \nStep 2:  Move the reverser to forward or reverse \nStep 3:  Release the brakes \nStep 4:   Communicate to your crew member that 3-point protection has  \nbeen released. \n23\nWhite line indicates the plane  \nof the rail.",
      "order": 24,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 25,
        "label": "Page 25"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cpkc_te_safety_rule_book_april_2023_p26_b0",
      "title": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023",
      "text": "Train & Engine Safety Rule Book - CPKC April 2023\nSafe Work Procedure: Applying 3-Point Protection in Remote \nControl Locomotive Service (RCLS / RCO)\nOnce a request for 3-point is received or 3-point is required: \nStep 1:  Ensure the speed selector is in “STOP” \nStep 2:  Ensure the reverser is in “NEUTRAL” \nStep 3:   Communicate to your crew member that 3-point protection has been applied.\nNOTE:  If an automatic brake is requested by a crew member or required by any \nother department i.e. Mechanical, Engineering etc., then at least a FULL \napplication must be made.\nT-28 Avoiding Slips, Trips or Falls\nTo avoid slips, trips or falls, employees must: \n• Remain alert and mindful of your surroundings at all times; \n• Use designated walkways, crosswalks, handholds and railings when available; \n• Plan and choose routes that afford the safest walking conditions; \n• Keep a clear view of where you are walking; \n• Avoid carrying objects that block your view; \n•  Keep locomotive cab, vehicle and other equipment floors clear of \nobstructions and tripping hazards; \n• For balance, keep hands out of pockets while walking. \n•  When transporting an end of train (EOT) device, the device hoses must  \nbe secured.\n24",
      "order": 25,
      "parent_id": null,
      "source_ref": {
        "page": 26,
        "label": "Page 26"
      }
    },
